{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1785676779351,"sessionID":"ses_03d6082b8ffevm2f5lmWdFLskw","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"bash","callID":"bash_11","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"cut -d, -f1 /Users/maui/.retort/work/retort-local-syu3u3ut/retort-02000dd611c2/data/kaggle/BR-Football-Dataset.csv | sort -u | head -30"},"output":"Copa do Brasil\nSerie A\nSerie B\nSerie C\ntournament\n","metadata":{"output":"Copa do Brasil\nSerie A\nSerie B\nSerie C\ntournament\n","exit":0,"truncated":false},"title":"cut -d, -f1 /Users/maui/.retort/work/retort-local-syu3u3ut/retort-02000dd611c2/data/kaggle/BR-Football-Dataset.csv | sort -u | head -30","time":{"start":1785676779348,"end":1785676779350}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"NuGet is accessible and ModelContextProtocol 2.0.0 is available (stable). Let me set up the project.\n\nPlan:\n1. Create solution + projects:\n   - `src/BrazilianSoccerMcp/BrazilianSoccerMcp.csproj` — net10.0 console app\n   - `tests/BrazilianSoccerMcp.Tests/BrazilianSoccerMcp.Tests.csproj` — xunit\n\n2. Core code:\n   - `Data/CsvParser.cs` — simple RFC-4180 CSV parser\n   - `Models/MatchRecord.cs`, `Models/PlayerRecord.cs`\n   - `Data/SoccerDataStore.cs` — loads all 6 CSVs into memory, normalized\n   - `Data/TeamNameNormalizer.cs` — normalize team names\n   - `Services/MatchQueryService.cs` — queries/stats\n   - `Services/PlayerQueryService.cs`\n   - `Tools/SoccerTools.cs` — MCP tool surface with [McpServerToolType]/[McpServerTool]\n\n3. Program.cs — host with stdio transport.\n\n4. Tests — xUnit with BDD-style names covering the five capability categories. The data path: tests need access to `data/kaggle` — I'll resolve path by walking up from the test assembly to find `data/kaggle`, or copy data. Better: environment-variable override + search upward for repo root.\n\nLet me check the ModelContextProtocol 2.0.0 API shape quickly. With the stable package, typical usage:\n\n```csharp\nusing Microsoft.Extensions.Hosting;\nusing ModelContextProtocol.Server;\n\nvar builder = Host.CreateApplicationBuilder(args);\nbuilder.Logging.AddConsole(options => options.LogToStandardErrorThreshold = LogLevel.Trace);\nbuilder.Services\n    .AddMcpServer()\n    .WithStdioServerTransport()\n    .WithToolsFromAssembly();\nawait builder.Build().RunAsync();\n```\n\nTools:\n\n```csharp\n[McpServerToolType]\npublic static class EchoTool\n{\n    [McpServerTool, Description(\"Echoes the message back to the client.\")]\n    public static string Echo(string message) => $\"hello {message}\";\n}\n```\n\nThat's the standard pattern. The package depends on Microsoft.Extensions.Hosting (via ModelContextProtocol's dependencies? Actually `ModelContextProtocol` package includes hosting support — `AddMcpServer` is in `Microsoft.Extensions.Hosting` namespace from the package `ModelContextProtocol`). Yes, the core `ModelContextProtocol` package includes `AddMcpServer` for HostApplicationBuilder.\n\nI need to be careful about versions: ModelContextProtocol 2.0.0 stable. Its API for tool attributes — `[McpServerToolType]`, `[McpServerTool]`, `[Description]` from `System.ComponentModel`. DI-injected services work: tool classes can be instance classes resolved from DI if registered. With `WithToolsFromAssembly()`, for instance (non-static) tool classes, they must be registered in DI... Actually the MCP SDK activates tool classes via the service provider; if not registered it may use ActivatorUtilities. In recent versions, `WithToolsFromAssembly` handles both static and instance classes; instance classes get created via `ActivatorUtilities.CreateInstance`. To be safe, I'll register my services in DI and use constructor injection in the tool class.\n\nFor the data store: load eagerly at startup (singleton) — 18k players + ~17k matches is small.\n\nFor AOT/trimming: not needed; keep it simple.\n\nLet me think about the team name normalization:\n- \"Palmeiras-SP\" → \"palmeiras\"\n- \"América - MG\" → \"america\"\n- \"Boavista Sport Club (antigo Esporte Clube Barreira) - RJ\" → \"boavista sport club\" (strip parenthetical and state)\n- Remove accents: \"Grêmio\" → \"gremio\", \"São Paulo\" → \"sao paulo\"\n- Also map common variations: \"Athletico-PR\" — base name \"athletico-pr\"? Hmm, \"Athletico-PR\" contains a state suffix \"-PR\" so it becomes \"athletico\". But in other datasets it's \"Athletico Paranaense\" or \"Atletico-PR\". Let me add alias mapping for common clubs:\n  - \"athletico paranaense\" / \"atletico pr\" / \"athletico pr\" / \"athletico-pr\" → \"athletico paranaense\"\n  - \"atletico mg\" / \"atletico mineiro\" → \"atletico mineiro\"\n  - \"atletico go\" / \"atletico goianiense\" → \"atletico goianiense\"\n  - \"botafogo rj\" vs \"botafogo\" — Botafogo de Futebol e Regatas (RJ) vs Botafogo-SP/PB. Keep \"botafogo\" matching careful... For simplicity, normalized key = base name after stripping state suffix and parentheticals; alias map for known variations.\n\nCareful with stripping \"-PR\" from \"Athletico-PR\": the base becomes \"athletico\" which then also matches... only itself. \"Sport-PE\" → \"sport\" (Sport Recife). OK.\n\nThe tricky part: stripping state suffix only when it's a known Brazilian state abbreviation (AC AL AP AM BA CE DF ES GO MA MT MS MG PA PB PR PE PI RJ RN RS RO RR SC SP SE TO) at the end, preceded by \"-\" or \" - \".\n\nDate parsing: multiple formats:\n- \"2012-05-19 18:30:00\" → yyyy-MM-dd HH:mm:ss\n- \"29/03/2003\" → dd/MM/yyyy\n- \"2023-09-24\" → yyyy-MM-dd\n\nUse DateTime.TryParseExact with multiple formats, culture invariant.\n\nFIFA data: columns with spaces in names (\"Jersey Number\"). I'll parse header row and index columns by name. Overall can be like \"94\". Some Overall fields might have \"88+2\"? No — Overall is plain; the position-specific ratings (LS, ST...) have \"88+2\" format. I'll parse Overall and Potential as ints. Age int. Position string. Height/Weight strings. Value strings. Keep it simple: store key fields + maybe skill ratings: Crossing, Finishing, Dribbling... The spec mentions \"Various skill ratings\". I'll expose a few main attributes: Crossing, Finishing, Dribbling, ShortPassing, BallControl, SprintSpeed, etc. Actually to keep it simple, I'll store Overall, Potential, Age, Position, Nationality, Club, Name, JerseyNumber, Height, Weight, PreferredFoot, WeakFoot, SkillMoves, and a handful of skills (Finishing, Dribbling, Crossing, ShortPassing, HeadingAccuracy, SprintSpeed, Stamina, Strength, GKDiving... ). Let me just grab a reasonable set.\n\nFor match queries across competitions:\n- Brasileirao_Matches.csv → competition \"Brasileirão Série A\"\n- Brazilian_Cup_Matches.csv → \"Copa do Brasil\"\n- Libertadores_Matches.csv → \"Copa Libertadores\"\n- BR-Football-Dataset.csv → tournament field varies (\"Copa do Brasil\", etc.) — includes many tournaments. Let me check distinct tournament values.\n- novo_campeonato_brasileiro.csv → \"Brasileirão (2003-2019)\" historical.\n\nLet me check the tournament values in BR-Football-Dataset and goal formats (they're floats \"1.0\").\n\nAlso check fifa_data clubs for Brazilian clubs — need to check names like \"Flamengo\" in the Club column.\n\nFor standings calculation: use Brasileirao_Matches.csv (round-robin 20-team seasons 2012+) and novo_campeonato_brasileiro (2003-2019). Points: 3/1/0. Sort by points, then wins, then goal difference, then goals for.\n\nNow for the tests — BDD style. I'll write xUnit tests with descriptive names like `FindMatchesBetweenTwoTeams_ReturnsMatchesWithDateScoresAndCompetition`. Also Reqnroll (SpecFlow successor) would be heavier; the spec says \"Use BDD test scenarios\" with gherkin examples. I could write tests in a Given/When/Then commenting style with plain xUnit — that's a reasonable interpretation. Keep it simple: xUnit with Given/When/Then comments. At least 20 sample questions need to be answerable — I'll write tests covering the sample questions from the spec.\n\nLet me check the tournaments in BR-Football-Dataset and teams in fifa data quickly.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_fc2a14986001ANmKcb9Li5ydgu","sessionID":"ses_03d6082b8ffevm2f5lmWdFLskw","messageID":"msg_fc2a0693d001uvcWjK397ZqNQE"}}
