{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1785676995281,"sessionID":"ses_03d6082b8ffevm2f5lmWdFLskw","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"bash","callID":"bash_15","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"python3 -c \"\nimport csv\n# cup rounds\nwith open('/Users/maui/.retort/work/retort-local-syu3u3ut/retort-02000dd611c2/data/kaggle/Brazilian_Cup_Matches.csv', encoding='utf-8') as f:\n    rounds = {}\n    for row in csv.DictReader(f):\n        rounds[row['round']] = rounds.get(row['round'], 0) + 1\nprint('cup rounds:', sorted(rounds.items(), key=lambda x: -x[1])[:15])\n# brasileirao seasons\nwith open('/Users/maui/.retort/work/retort-local-syu3u3ut/retort-02000dd611c2/data/kaggle/Brasileirao_Matches.csv', encoding='utf-8') as f:\n    seasons = {}\n    for row in csv.DictReader(f):\n        seasons[row['season']] = seasons.get(row['season'], 0) + 1\nprint('brasileirao seasons:', sorted(seasons.items()))\n\""},"output":"cup rounds: [('2', 376), ('1', 334), ('3', 258), ('4', 140), ('5', 123), ('6', 62), ('7', 30), ('8', 14)]\nbrasileirao seasons: [('2012', 380), ('2013', 380), ('2014', 380), ('2015', 380), ('2016', 380), ('2017', 380), ('2018', 380), ('2019', 380), ('2020', 380), ('2021', 380), ('2022', 380)]\n","metadata":{"output":"cup rounds: [('2', 376), ('1', 334), ('3', 258), ('4', 140), ('5', 123), ('6', 62), ('7', 30), ('8', 14)]\nbrasileirao seasons: [('2012', 380), ('2013', 380), ('2014', 380), ('2015', 380), ('2016', 380), ('2017', 380), ('2018', 380), ('2019', 380), ('2020', 380), ('2021', 380), ('2022', 380)]\n","exit":0,"truncated":false},"title":"python3 -c \"\nimport csv\n# cup rounds\nwith open('/Users/maui/.retort/work/retort-local-syu3u3ut/retort-02000dd611c2/data/kaggle/Brazilian_Cup_Matches.csv', encoding='utf-8') as f:\n    rounds = {}\n    for row in csv.DictReader(f):\n        rounds[row['round']] = rounds.get(row['round'], 0) + 1\nprint('cup rounds:', sorted(rounds.items(), key=lambda x: -x[1])[:15])\n# brasileirao seasons\nwith open('/Users/maui/.retort/work/retort-local-syu3u3ut/retort-02000dd611c2/data/kaggle/Brasileirao_Matches.csv', encoding='utf-8') as f:\n    seasons = {}\n    for row in csv.DictReader(f):\n        seasons[row['season']] = seasons.get(row['season'], 0) + 1\nprint('brasileirao seasons:', sorted(seasons.items()))\n\"","time":{"start":1785676995276,"end":1785676995279}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"The FIFA data is FIFA 19 — 827 Brazilian players, and Brazilian clubs include Grêmio, Atlético Mineiro, Cruzeiro, Fluminense, Santos, Internacional, etc. (the FIFA 19 \"Rest of World\" Brazilian teams). Flamengo/Palmeiras/Corinthians/São Paulo aren't in this FIFA 19 export.\n\nNow I have a clear picture. Let me design and build:\n\n**Project layout:**\n```\nBrazilianSoccerMcp.sln\nsrc/BrazilianSoccerMcp/\n  BrazilianSoccerMcp.csproj\n  Program.cs\n  Data/CsvParser.cs\n  Data/DataPathResolver.cs\n  Data/SoccerDataStore.cs\n  Data/TeamNameNormalizer.cs\n  Models/MatchRecord.cs\n  Models/PlayerRecord.cs\n  Services/MatchQueryService.cs\n  Services/PlayerQueryService.cs\n  Services/StandingsCalculator.cs (fold into MatchQueryService)\n  Tools/SoccerTools.cs\ntests/BrazilianSoccerMcp.Tests/\n  BrazilianSoccerMcp.Tests.csproj\n  Fixtures/DataStoreFixture.cs\n  MatchQueryTests.cs\n  TeamStatsTests.cs\n  PlayerQueryTests.cs\n  CompetitionTests.cs\n  StatisticsTests.cs\n  NormalizerTests.cs\n```\n\n**Data store** loads all 6 CSVs:\n- MatchRecord: Competition, Date (DateOnly/DateTime), HomeTeam, AwayTeam, HomeGoals, AwayGoals, Season, RoundOrStage, SourceFile. Plus normalized home/away keys.\n- Sources:\n  1. Brasileirao_Matches.csv → competition \"Brasileirão Série A\", round number\n  2. Brazilian_Cup_Matches.csv → \"Copa do Brasil\", round text\n  3. Libertadores_Matches.csv → \"Copa Libertadores\", stage\n  4. BR-Football-Dataset.csv → tournament (\"Serie A\"/\"Serie B\"/\"Serie C\"/\"Copa do Brasil\") + extra stats (corners/shots/attacks). I'll add optional stats fields.\n  5. novo_campeonato_brasileiro.csv → \"Brasileirão (Histórico 2003–2019)\", round, arena.\n- PlayerRecord: Id, Name, Age, Nationality, Overall, Potential, Club, Position, JerseyNumber, Height, Weight, PreferredFoot, skills (Finishing, Dribbling, etc.)\n\nCompetition naming: I want matching by competition query (\"brasileirao\", \"copa do brasil\", \"libertadores\") to work across sources. I'll give each match a `Competition` (display) and `CompetitionKey` (normalized: \"brasileirao-serie-a\", \"copa-do-brasil\", \"copa-libertadores\", \"brasileirao-serie-b\", \"brasileirao-serie-c\"). For BR-Football-Dataset \"Serie A\" → key \"brasileirao-serie-a\", display \"Brasileirão Série A (BR-Football)\". Hmm — but then \"brasileirao\" queries should match both \"brasileirao-serie-a\" and historical. I'll make competition matching: normalized substring/fuzzy — competition key contains query key. Query \"brasileirao\" matches \"brasileirao-serie-a\" and \"brasileiro-historico\"? Let me define competition keys:\n- \"brasileirao-serie-a\" (Brasileirao_Matches.csv)\n- \"copa-do-brasil\" (Brazilian_Cup_Matches.csv)\n- \"copa-libertadores\" (Libertadores_Matches.csv)\n- from BR-Football: \"brasileirao-serie-a\", \"brasileirao-serie-b\", \"brasileirao-serie-c\", \"copa-do-brasil\" (source-tagged)\n- \"campeonato-brasileiro\" for novo_campeonato_brasileiro (historical). Hmm, \"brasileirao\" wouldn't match \"campeonato-brasileiro\". Better key: \"brasileirao-historico-2003-2019\". Then query \"brasileirao\" matches it too. Good.\n\nCompetition matching: normalize query (remove accents, lowercase) and check `key.Contains(queryKey)` OR display-name-normalized contains. \"serie a\" → \"serie-a\"? Normalization replaces spaces with... I'll normalize both to accent-free lowercase and compare with contains after removing non-alphanumerics? \"brasileirão\" → \"brasileirao\"; key \"brasileirao-serie-a\" contains \"brasileirao\" ✓. \"copa do brasil\" → key contains \"copa-do-brasil\" if spaces→dash. I'll normalize spaces to dashes for keys and compare. Also alias \"libertadores\" → matches \"copa-libertadores\" ✓.\n\n**Team name normalization:**\n```csharp\nNormalize(name):\n  - trim\n  - strip parenthetical \"(...)\" parts\n  - strip trailing state suffix: \"-XX\" or \" - XX\" where XX in states set\n  - remove accents (FormD, strip NonSpacingMark)\n  - lowercase\n  - collapse whitespace\n  - apply alias map\n```\nAliases (normalized input → canonical):\n- \"atletico pr\" / \"athletico pr\" / \"athletico paranaense\" / \"atletico paranaense\" / \"athletico\" → \"athletico paranaense\"\n- \"atletico mg\" / \"atletico mineiro\" → \"atletico mineiro\"\n- \"atletico go\" / \"atletico goianiense\" → \"atletico goianiense\"\n- \"america mg\" / \"america mineiro\" / \"america fc\" / \"america fc minas gerais\" → \"america mineiro\"\n- \"sport\" / \"sport recife\" / \"sport club do recife\" → \"sport recife\"\n- \"gremio\" → \"gremio\" (canonical without accent)\n- \"athletico-go\"? exists \"Atlético-GO\" in some data. Hmm, \"atletico-go\" after stripping state suffix \"-GO\" → \"atletico\" — collision! \"Athletico-PR\" → \"athletico\" too, and plain \"Atlético\"? The state-strip would turn both \"Athletico-PR\" and \"Atlético-GO\" into \"atletico\". To avoid collision, alias map handles: raw \"athletico-pr\" → hmm. \n\nBetter approach: FIRST check alias on the raw normalized full string (before state stripping), THEN strip state, then check alias again on stripped.\n\nOrder:\n1. normalize basic (accents, lowercase, trim, collapse spaces)\n2. if alias contains full → done\n3. strip parenthetical\n4. if alias contains → done\n5. strip state suffix (only if the remaining base is non-empty and not making it ambiguous)\n6. if alias contains → done\n7. return stripped\n\nAlias keys (accent-free lowercase):\n- \"athletico-pr\" → \"athletico paranaense\"\n- \"athletico paranaense\" → itself\n- \"atletico paranaense\" → \"athletico paranaense\"\n- \"atletico-pr\" → \"athletico paranaense\"\n- \"atletico-go\" → \"atletico goianiense\"\n- \"athletico-go\" → \"atletico goianiense\"\n- \"atletico goianiense\" → itself\n- \"atletico-mg\" → \"atletico mineiro\"\n- \"atletico mineiro\" → itself\n- \"america-mg\" → \"america mineiro\"\n- \"america mineiro\" → itself\n- \"america fc\" → \"america mineiro\"\n- \"america fc (minas gerais)\" → after paren-strip → \"america fc\" → alias\n- \"sport club do recife\" → \"sport recife\"\n- \"sport recife\" → itself\n- \"sport-pe\" → \"sport recife\"? Hmm wait — state-strip of \"Sport-PE\" gives \"sport\". Alias \"sport\" → \"sport recife\" is safe (no other Brazilian \"Sport\" club).\n- \"vasco\" / \"vasco da gama\" → \"vasco da gama\"\n- \"sao paulo\" → itself\n- \"inter de limeira\"? skip.\n- \"boavista sport club\" — the cup data has \"Boavista Sport Club (antigo Esporte Clube Barreira) - RJ\" → paren-strip → \"boavista sport club\" + strip \"- RJ\" — wait order: strip paren first, then state → \"boavista sport club\". OK.\n- \"parana\" (Paraná Clube) — fine.\n- \"ceara sporting club\" / \"ceara\" → \"ceara\"\n- \"vitoria\" fine.\n- \"botafogo\" — in FIFA data Botafogo = Botafogo FR (RJ). In match data \"Botafogo-RJ\" → \"botafogo\". There's also \"Botafogo-SP\"/\"Botafogo-PB\" → \"botafogo\" too after state strip! Collision: \"Botafogo-SP\" (a different club) vs \"Botafogo-RJ\". Hmm. This is a known data-quality tradeoff. To handle: keep state as disambiguator when present. I'll build the normalized key as: base name; and separately store state when available. Match queries match on base-name equality; for queries like \"Botafogo\" this merges RJ/SP/PB clubs — acceptable for a demo but I can do better: alias known ones: \"botafogo-rj\" → \"botafogo\", \"botafogo-sp\" → \"botafogo sp\", \"botafogo-pb\" → \"botafogo pb\". Similarly \"river-pi\" vs River Plate? \"River Plate\" (URU) appears in Libertadores. State suffix only strips Brazilian states; \"-PI\" is a Brazilian state (Piauí). \"River Plate\" has no suffix so stays \"river plate\" distinct from \"river\". OK.\n\nAlso \"Figueirense\", \"Avai\" fine. \"Internacional\" → \"internacional\"; FIFA \"Internacional\" same ✓.\n\nNote Libertadores has foreign teams: \"Nacional (URU)\" → paren-strip → \"nacional\". \"Barcelona-EQU\" — \"-EQU\" is not a Brazilian state so no strip → \"barcelona-equ\". Good, distinct from \"Barcelona\". Hmm \"Nacional (URU)\" vs \"Nacional (PAR)\"? Both → \"nacional\" collision, minor, acceptable for demo.\n\nAlso handle \"América - MG\" (with spaces around dash) → alias \"america - mg\"? My normalization: strip paren, collapse spaces, then state-suffix regex `\\s*-\\s*(AC|AL|...)$` → \"america\" — hmm then \"america\" ≠ \"america mineiro\". Add alias \"america\" → \"america mineiro\"? There's also \"América-RN\" (América de Natal)... In the cup dataset, \"América - MG\" appears. State-aware aliasing: since regex strips state, I lose info. Better: BEFORE stripping state, check the full normalized string (with state) against alias map: \"america - mg\" normalized... I'll normalize dashes: replace \" - \" with \"-\"? Let me define normalization carefully:\n\n```\ns = name.Trim()\ns = remove parentheticals: regex \"\\s*\\([^)]*\\)\"\ns = s.RemoveDiacritics().ToLowerInvariant()\ns = collapse whitespace\n// alias pass 1 on full string with separators normalized:\nkey1 = s with \" - \" → \"-\", spaces→\"\" hmm\n```\n\nSimpler: build TWO keys:\n- `fullKey`: normalized with dashes/accents removed: lowercase, remove accents, replace [' ', '-', '_'] runs with nothing? No — that would merge \"sao paulo\" → \"saopaulo\". That's actually fine as a lookup key! As long as both data and queries go through the same normalizer, \"saopaulo\" as internal key is fine.\n\nSo: `key = new string(name.RemoveDiacritics().ToLower().Where(char.IsLetterOrDigit).ToArray())` — strips spaces, dashes, parens content? No, parens content must be removed BEFORE (since \"(antigo Esporte Clube Barreira)\" shouldn't be part of key, but \"(URU)\" also removed — fine).\n\nThen alias map on these compact keys:\n- \"athleticopr\" → \"athleticoparanaense\"\n- \"atleticopr\" → \"athleticoparanaense\"\n- \"atleticoparanaense\" → \"athleticoparanaense\"\n- \"athletico\" (from \"Athletico-PR\" after... wait, no state stripping needed anymore? \n\nHmm — if I don't strip the state suffix, \"Palmeiras-SP\" → \"palmeirassp\" but \"Palmeiras\" → \"palmeiras\". Query \"Palmeiras\" must match both. So state-strip IS needed. With compact keys, state-strip = remove trailing state abbreviation letters... \"palmeirassp\" — remove trailing \"sp\"? Dangerous: \"santos\" ends in \"s\"… only strip exact known suffixes from the compact key: the compact key ends with one of the 2-letter state codes — \"palmeirassp\" ends with \"sp\" → \"palmeiras\". But \"santos\"... doesn't end with a state code. \"vasco\" no. \"gremio\" no. \"athleticopr\" ends with \"pr\" → \"athletico\" — collision again between PR and GO versions (\"atleticogo\" → \"atletico\"). Handle via alias BEFORE state strip: alias check on full compact key first (\"athleticopr\" → \"athleticoparanaense\"), then strip state, then alias again (\"america\" → \"americamineiro\", \"sport\" → \"sportrecife\", \"athletico\" → \"athleticoparanaense\" since remaining \"athletico\" comes from either \"athleticopr\"(already aliased) or... \"atleticogo\" is aliased first to \"atleticogoianiense\", so bare \"atletico\" only from weird cases; map \"atletico\" → ambiguous, leave as-is).\n\nAnd \"botafogosp\" → alias-first? \"botafogosp\" not in alias → state-strip → \"botafogo\". Collision RJ/SP. Add aliases: \"botafogosp\" → \"botafogosp\"(keep, distinct), i.e., alias maps full→full for these: \"botafogorj\" → \"botafogo\", \"botafogosp\" stays \"botafogosp\", \"botafogopb\" stays. So state-strip for \"botafogo*\" variants: only strip when result is in alias or just... I'll implement: if alias contains fullKey → return alias; compute stripped = fullKey minus state suffix if applicable; if alias contains stripped → return alias; return stripped ?? fullKey.\n\nFor \"botafogosp\": alias check full → no. stripped \"botafogo\" → alias contains \"botafogo\"? I won't put \"botafogo\" in the alias map (it's a plain canonical name). Return \"botafogo\". Collision remains. To prevent, add explicit aliases: \"botafogosp\" → \"botafogosp\" — i.e., handle BEFORE state stripping via a \"keep-state\" set: clubs whose base name is ambiguous across states: botafogo (RJ/SP/PB), river? Actually simpler: alias map entries \"botafogosp\"→\"botafogo-sp\", \"botafogopb\"→\"botafogo-pb\" mapping to distinct canonicals that aren't reachable otherwise. Then \"botafogorj\" → strip → \"botafogo\". Query \"Botafogo\" returns RJ club (and FIFA Botafogo = RJ ✓). Query \"Botafogo-SP\" → normalizes full → \"botafogosp\" → alias → \"botafogo-sp\" distinct ✓. \n\nBut wait — matching is exact-key equality. Should \"botafogo\" query ALSO match \"botafogo-sp\"? For head-to-head \"Flamengo vs Botafogo\" the user means RJ. Exact-key keeps them separate — good semantics. Partial matching could be a fallback: if exact key yields no matches and the query key is a prefix/substring of team keys, offer substring matching? Keep exact for team A vs team B; for single-team search, use exact too but if no exact key exists among teams, fall back to substring on display names. Hmm — actually let me make team matching: exact normalized-key match; PLUS substring candidates when exact fails (e.g. query \"corinthians\" matches \"sportclubcorinthianspaulista\"? No — \"corinthians\" is the base name in the CSVs anyway: \"Corinthians-SP\" → \"corinthians\" ✓).\n\nWhat about FIFA clubs — \"Sport Club do Recife\" → compact \"sportclubdorecife\" → alias → \"sportrecife\" ✓ (matches match-data \"Sport-PE\" → \"sport\" → alias \"sportrecife\" ✓). \"Atlético Mineiro\" FIFA → \"atleticomineiro\" ✓. \"América FC (Minas Gerais)\" → strip paren → \"americafc\" → alias → \"americamineiro\" ✓. \"Paraná\" → \"parana\" ✓ (matches \"Paraná-PR\" → \"parana\" ✓). \"Ceará Sporting Club\" → \"cearasportingclub\" → alias → \"ceara\" ✓. \"Grêmio\" → \"gremio\" ✓.\n\nGood, that design works. I'll include a reasonable alias table.\n\n**Tools (MCP surface)** — I'll expose these tools:\n1. `find_matches(team, opponent?, competition?, season?, from_date?, to_date?, home_or_away?, limit?)` — list matches\n2. `head_to_head(team_a, team_b, competition?)` — summary + match list\n3. `team_record(team, season?, competition?, venue? home/away)` — W/D/L, goals\n4. `team_competitions(team)` — which competitions/seasons a team appears in\n5. `competition_standings(competition, season)` — computed table\n6. `list_competitions()` — available competitions + seasons + match counts\n7. `biggest_wins(competition?, season?, limit?)` — largest margins\n8. `goal_statistics(competition?, season?)` — avg goals, home win rate, etc.\n9. `search_players(name?, nationality?, club?, position?, min_overall?, sort?, limit?)`\n10. `get_player(name)` — best match details\n11. `club_players(club, ...)`, could fold into search_players with club filter. Keep search_players only.\n12. `top_players(nationality?, club?, position?, limit?)` — fold into search_players with sort=overall desc default. Keep both? Keep `search_players` + `get_player` to limit surface. Actually for \"highest-rated players at Flamengo\" search_players(club:\"Flamengo\", sort:\"overall\") works.\n\nI'll implement 10 tools. All return formatted strings (LLM-friendly text like the spec examples).\n\n**Dates**: store DateOnly? Some matches have time. Store DateTime date + optional time. For output \"2023-09-03\" format. Sorting by date.\n\nBR-Football-Dataset date is \"2023-09-24\" with separate \"time\" \"20:00:00\". Goals are floats \"1.0\" → parse as double → int.\n\nnovo_campeonato: date \"29/03/2003\" dd/MM/yyyy. Also has goals as ints. Vencedor field. Arena. OBS column exists.\n\nNote the historical CSV: check encoding — likely UTF-8. Also check for a stray \"OBS\" column and possible malformed rows. My CSV parser must handle quotes and be tolerant of short rows.\n\n**Season**: int? Brasileirao_Matches season 2012..2023 probably. BR-Football has no season column — derive from date year. Libertadores has season. Cup has season. Historical has Ano.\n\n**Standings calculation**: For \"2019 Brasileirão\": which source? Brasileirao_Matches.csv covers 2012-2023, historical covers 2003-2019. Standings tool: pick matches from competition key \"brasileirao-serie-a\" (both Brasileirao_Matches + BR-Football Serie A + historical). Problem: duplicates across sources! Brasileirao_Matches 2019 and historical 2019 and BR-Football Serie A 2019 overlap. For standings, I must dedupe or pick one source per season. Strategy: prefer Brasileirao_Matches (ricardomattos) for seasons it covers; else historical; else BR-Football. Or dedupe by (date, homeKey, awayKey). Dedupe by (home, away, season) could merge double round-robin legit duplicates (home/away pairs are unique per season in Brasileirão — each pair plays twice, once at each home, so (home,away,season) IS unique in Serie A). Copa do Brasil has two-legged ties so (home,away,season) NOT unique there. \n\nSimplest robust approach: standings use a per-season source preference: choose the source with the most complete data for (competitionKey, season): group matches by source file, pick the group with max matches for that season, but if two sources both have ≥90% of max, still pick one (the preferred). Preference order: Brasileirao_Matches.csv > novo_campeonato_brasileiro.csv > BR-Football-Dataset.csv. For Copa do Brasil: Brazilian_Cup_Matches.csv preferred over BR-Football. That avoids double counting.\n\nActually simpler and deterministic: assign each match a SourceRank; standings/season-stat queries that aggregate over a competition+season use only the best source for that competition+season (rank then count). I'll implement `SelectPrimarySource(matches)` per (competitionKey, season) group: order by sourceRank asc, count desc → take first source's matches.\n\nHmm, but what if Brasileirao_Matches has only partial 2024 season while BR-Football has full? rank picks Brasileirao anyway. Fine for demo — but let me instead pick by count when counts differ a lot... Keep it simple: pick source with (rank asc). Deterministic, documented.\n\nActually wait — let me reconsider: pure rank might give incomplete seasons. Pick by count desc, tie-break rank asc. If Brasileirao_Matches 2023 has 380 matches and BR-Football Serie A 2023 has 380 — tie → rank picks Brasileirao_Matches ✓. If one has fewer, the bigger set wins ✓. Good.\n\n**Program.cs**: stdio MCP server. Also support a `--print-tools` smoke mode? Not needed.\n\n**Tests**: xUnit. Fixture loads data store once (path resolution: walk up from AppContext.BaseDirectory to find \"data/kaggle\"). Tests cover:\n- CSV parser (quoted fields)\n- Normalizer (state suffix, accents, aliases)\n- BDD scenarios for each of the 5 capability areas, incl. ~20 sample questions:\n  1. Flamengo vs Fluminense matches exist with date/scores/competition\n  2. Palmeiras matches in 2023\n  3. Copa do Brasil finals (round contains \"final\")? Cup data round values — check distinct rounds.\n  4. Corinthians home record 2022 (wins+draws+losses = 19)\n  5. Most goals in Serie A 2023 (team)\n  6. Palmeiras vs Santos head-to-head\n  7. Brazilian players count = 827\n  8. Highest-rated Brazilian = Neymar (FIFA19: Neymar 92)\n  9. Forwards from São Paulo — São Paulo FC not in FIFA clubs! Use Santos instead. Position filter \"ST\".\n  10. 2019 Brasileirão champion = Flamengo, 90 pts\n  11. Relegated 2020? — historical data ends 2019; Brasileirao_Matches has 2020. Relegation = bottom 4 of standings. Test computes bottom 4 of 2019: Cruzeiro, CSA, Chapecoense, Avaí — good check: 2019 relegated were Cruzeiro, CSA, Chapecoense, Avaí ✓ (famous Cruzeiro relegation).\n  12. Avg goals per match in Brasileirão\n  13. Best away record\n  14. Biggest wins\n  15. Last Flamengo vs Corinthians\n  16. Gabriel Barbosa search → \"Gabriel Barbosa\" in FIFA data? He's Brazilian, at Inter? In FIFA 19 Gabi was at Inter (on loan from Inter Milan?). FIFA 19: Gabriel Barbosa club = \"Inter\" (Milan). Search by name should find him.\n  17. Players at Flamengo → FIFA has no Flamengo → returns 0 with helpful message. Test Grêmio instead (20 players).\n  18. Competitions Palmeiras played → multiple competitions list\n  19. Compare 2018 vs 2019 seasons (aggregates differ, match counts 380 each)\n  20. Team name variants: \"Palmeiras-SP\" and \"Palmeiras\" match same team\n  21. UTF-8 names: \"São Paulo\", \"Grêmio\" queries work\n  22. Date range filter\n  23. Cross-file: player + club (Santos players + Santos matches)\n\nAlso performance sanity: queries < 2s — trivially satisfied with in-memory data; add a perf smoke test.\n\nLet me check cup \"round\" distinct values and Brasileirao seasons range, and check 2019 standings from Brasileirao_Matches to confirm Flamengo 90pts computable. Also confirm FIFA Neymar overall 92 and Gabriel Barbosa presence.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_fc2a4846300122rx9HRqHMGVW2","sessionID":"ses_03d6082b8ffevm2f5lmWdFLskw","messageID":"msg_fc2a1c7cb001Zm6cuSVta5O6X0"}}
