', '2015') 378\n('Serie A', '2016') 379\n('Serie A', '2017') 380\n('Serie A', '2018') 380\n('Serie A', '2019') 380\n('Serie A', '2020') 264\n('Serie A', '2021') 491\n('Serie A', '2022') 379\n('Serie A', '2023') 377\n('Serie B', '2014') 275\n('Serie B', '2015') 378\n('Serie B', '2016') 380\n('Serie B', '2017') 381\n('Serie B', '2018') 377\n('Serie B', '2019') 378\n('Serie B', '2020') 302\n('Serie B', '2021') 446\n('Serie B', '2022') 380\n('Serie B', '2023') 380\n('Serie C', '2014') 81\n('Serie C', '2015') 157\n('Serie C', '2016') 181\n('Serie C', '2017') 179\n('Serie C', '2018') 192\n('Serie C', '2019') 194\n('Serie C', '2020') 179\n('Serie C', '2021') 216\n('Serie C', '2022') 213\n('Serie C', '2023') 215\n","exit":0,"truncated":false},"title":"python3 -c \"\nimport csv\nfrom collections import Counter\nwith open('/Users/maui/.retort/work/retort-local-syu3u3ut/retort-02000dd611c2/data/kaggle/BR-Football-Dataset.csv', encoding='utf-8') as f:\n    rows = list(csv.DictReader(f))\nc = Counter((r['tournament'], r['date'][:4]) for r in rows)\nfor k in sorted(c): print(k, c[k])\n\" | head -50","time":{"start":1785677033019,"end":1785677033022}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"Interesting findings:\n1. Cup rounds are numeric (1-8), not text. \"Final\" would be round 8 in most seasons (or the last round played per season). So \"Copa do Brasil finals\" = matches in the max round per season. Hmm, round numbers per season may vary (some seasons start at round 1, some at round 2?). Let me check per-season max rounds later — for the tool, I'll treat \"final\" as the highest round number in that season for the cup. Actually simpler: the `find_matches` tool can filter by round/stage text; and for finals specifically I can note it. Not critical — I'll handle \"final\" by matching max-round per season when competition is Copa do Brasil? That's speculative. Simpler: expose `stage`/`round` filter as string; document that cup rounds are numeric 1-8 where the max is the final. For a \"finals\" query I could add a convenience: in find_matches, if round filter is \"final\", resolve per season max round. Nice touch, doable.\n\nWait — actually the rounds seem off: round '8' has 14 matches total across all seasons (2012-2023 = 12 seasons ≈ 1-2 matches each — final is 2 legs, some seasons 1?). Round '2' has 376?? That seems like round numbering may count from the final? 376/12 seasons ≈ 31 matches per season — that's round-of-32? Hmm, Copa do Brasil has ~80-90 matches per season. Total 1337/12 ≈ 111. Whatever — I'll compute \"final\" per season as max round.\n\nHmm wait, actually round '2' with 376 matches across 12 seasons = 31.3 per season. A round with 31 matches can't be right for a knockout... unless early rounds have many teams. Copa do Brasil 2012 had 64 teams; round 1 = 32 matches? Then round '1' 334/12 ≈ 28, round '2' ≈ 31. Plausible if rounds progress 1→8 with fewer matches each — but round 2 > round 1 in count. Eh, maybe some seasons numbered differently. Doesn't matter for the implementation.\n\n2. Gabriel Barbosa is NOT in the FIFA dataset (empty result). OK — test search by partial name \"Gabriel\" returns Brazilians like Gabriel Jesus, Gabriel Paulista etc. For \"Who is Gabriel Barbosa?\" the tool returns no exact match but suggests similar names. I'll implement get_player with fuzzy fallback: exact → substring → no match message with suggestions. Test uses a player that exists (e.g. \"Gabriel Jesus\" or \"Neymar\").\n\n3. Brasileirao_Matches.csv: 2012-2022 only (11 seasons, 380 each). No 2023! The spec example mentions \"Palmeiras play in 2023\" — for 2023 Serie A, BR-Football-Dataset has Serie A 2023. Let me verify BR-Football seasons per tournament.\n\nAlso historical novo_campeonato: 2003-2019. So Serie A coverage: 2003-2019 (historical), 2012-2022 (Brasileirao_Matches), and BR-Football (Serie A which years?). Let me check.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_fc2a5232f001HVvBmHzIwx