{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1785677154506,"sessionID":"ses_03d6082b8ffevm2f5lmWdFLskw","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"bash","callID":"bash_19","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"python3 -c \"\nimport csv, re, unicodedata\ndef norm(s):\n    s = re.sub(r'\\s*\\([^)]*\\)', '', s).strip()\n    s = ''.join(c for c in unicodedata.normalize('NFD', s) if unicodedata.category(c) != 'Mn')\n    return s.lower()\nrows = list(csv.DictReader(open('/Users/maui/.retort/work/retort-local-syu3u3ut/retort-02000dd611c2/data/kaggle/Brasileirao_Matches.csv', encoding='utf-8')))\n# Corinthians home 2022\nh = [r for r in rows if r['season']=='2022' and norm(r['home_team']).startswith('corinthians')]\nw = sum(1 for r in h if int(r['home_goal'])>int(r['away_goal']))\nd = sum(1 for r in h if r['home_goal']==r['away_goal'])\nl = len(h)-w-d\ngf = sum(int(r['home_goal']) for r in h); ga = sum(int(r['away_goal']) for r in h)\nprint('Corinthians home 2022:', len(h), w, d, l, gf, ga)\n# Fla-Flu\nff = [r for r in rows if {norm(r['home_team']), norm(r['away_team'])} == {'flamengo-rj','fluminense-rj'}]\nprint('Fla-Flu in Brasileirao_Matches:', len(ff), ff[-1]['datetime'] if ff else None)\n# 2019 standings check\nfrom collections import defaultdict\npts = defaultdict(lambda: [0,0,0,0,0,0])\nfor r in rows:\n    if r['season']!='2019': continue\n    ht, at = r['home_team'], r['away_team']; hg, ag = int(r['home_goal']), int(r['away_goal'])\n    pts[ht][0]+= hg>ag and 3 or hg==ag and 1 or 0; pts[at][0]+= ag>hg and 3 or hg==ag and 1 or 0\ntop = sorted(pts.items(), key=lambda kv:-kv[1][0])[:3]\nprint('2019 top3:', top)\n\""},"output":"Traceback (most recent call last):\n  File \"<string>\", line 10, in <module>\n  File \"<string>\", line 10, in <genexpr>\nValueError: invalid literal for int() with base 10: 'NA'\n","metadata":{"output":"Traceback (most recent call last):\n  File \"<string>\", line 10, in <module>\n  File \"<string>\", line 10, in <genexpr>\nValueError: invalid literal for int() with base 10: 'NA'\n","exit":1,"truncated":false},"title":"python3 -c \"\nimport csv, re, unicodedata\ndef norm(s):\n    s = re.sub(r'\\s*\\([^)]*\\)', '', s).strip()\n    s = ''.join(c for c in unicodedata.normalize('NFD', s) if unicodedata.category(c) != 'Mn')\n    return s.lower()\nrows = list(csv.DictReader(open('/Users/maui/.retort/work/retort-local-syu3u3ut/retort-02000dd611c2/data/kaggle/Brasileirao_Matches.csv', encoding='utf-8')))\n# Corinthians home 2022\nh = [r for r in rows if r['season']=='2022' and norm(r['home_team']).startswith('corinthians')]\nw = sum(1 for r in h if int(r['home_goal'])>int(r['away_goal']))\nd = sum(1 for r in h if r['home_goal']==r['away_goal'])\nl = len(h)-w-d\ngf = sum(int(r['home_goal']) for r in h); ga = sum(int(r['away_goal']) for r in h)\nprint('Corinthians home 2022:', len(h), w, d, l, gf, ga)\n# Fla-Flu\nff = [r for r in rows if {norm(r['home_team']), norm(r['away_team'])} == {'flamengo-rj','fluminense-rj'}]\nprint('Fla-Flu in Brasileirao_Matches:', len(ff), ff[-1]['datetime'] if ff else None)\n# 2019 standings check\nfrom collections import defaultdict\npts = defaultdict(lambda: [0,0,0,0,0,0])\nfor r in rows:\n    if r['season']!='2019': continue\n    ht, at = r['home_team'], r['away_team']; hg, ag = int(r['home_goal']), int(r['away_goal'])\n    pts[ht][0]+= hg>ag and 3 or hg==ag and 1 or 0; pts[at][0]+= ag>hg and 3 or hg==ag and 1 or 0\ntop = sorted(pts.items(), key=lambda kv:-kv[1][0])[:3]\nprint('2019 top3:', top)\n\"","time":{"start":1785677154499,"end":1785677154502}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"Important observations:\n- BR-Football Serie A 2020 has 264 matches, 2021 has 491 (!) — more than 380, meaning the tournament column includes extra matches (maybe state championships mixed in, or 2021 count includes 2020 season spillover played in 2021... 491 > 380 means it's NOT a clean single-season set). So my \"pick source by max count\" heuristic would wrongly pick BR-Football for 2021 (491 > 380 from Brasileirao_Matches). Rank-based preference is safer: prefer the dedicated source (Brasileirao_Matches for Serie A) and only fall back to BR-Football when the dedicated source lacks that season (e.g., 2023 Serie A → BR-Football 377 matches ≈ full season; 2020 → Brasileirao_Matches has 380 ✓).\n\nSo source preference per competition:\n- brasileirao-serie-a: rank 1 = Brasileirao_Matches.csv, rank 2 = novo_campeonato_brasileiro.csv, rank 3 = BR-Football-Dataset.csv. For season S, use the highest-ranked source that HAS matches for S.\n- copa-do-brasil: rank 1 = Brazilian_Cup_Matches.csv, rank 2 = BR-Football.\n- copa-libertadores: only Libertadores_Matches.csv.\n- serie-b/serie-c: only BR-Football.\n\nThis gives: Serie A standings 2003-2011 → historical; 2012-2022 → Brasileirao_Matches; 2023 → BR-Football. \n\n- Libertadores has \"NA\" season rows — handle gracefully (season null).\n- Cup final: max round per season (2012: 6, 2016: 7, 2021: 4 — data partial). \"Final\" filter = max round within that season.\n\nNow, let me also confirm: does historical novo_campeonato have goals as plain ints and dates dd/MM/yyyy? Yes from the head sample. Encoding might be latin-1? \"Grêmio\" appeared correctly in my terminal read. Let me check file encoding quickly. Actually the `head` output showed \"Grêmio\" correctly, so UTF-8 it is.\n\nFIFA data: Overall is int. Position single code like \"RF\", \"RCB\", \"CDM\". Position filter: exact position code match, or position-group filter (forward/midfielder/defender/goalkeeper)? I'll support both: if query is a group name, map to position sets; else exact code.\n\nPosition groups:\n- GK: GK\n- Defender: LB, LCB, CB, RCB, RB, LWB, RWB\n- Midfielder: LM, LCM, CM, RCM, RM, LDM, CDM, RDM, LAM, CAM, RAM\n- Forward: LW, RW, LF, CF, RF, ST, LS, RS\n\nNow write the code. Structure:\n\n```\nBrazilianSoccerMcp.sln\nsrc/BrazilianSoccerMcp/BrazilianSoccerMcp.csproj\nsrc/BrazilianSoccerMcp/Program.cs\nsrc/BrazilianSoccerMcp/Data/CsvParser.cs\nsrc/BrazilianSoccerMcp/Data/DataPathResolver.cs\nsrc/BrazilianSoccerMcp/Data/TeamNameNormalizer.cs\nsrc/BrazilianSoccerMcp/Data/SoccerDataStore.cs\nsrc/BrazilianSoccerMcp/Models/MatchRecord.cs\nsrc/BrazilianSoccerMcp/Models/PlayerRecord.cs\nsrc/BrazilianSoccerMcp/Services/MatchQueryService.cs\nsrc/BrazilianSoccerMcp/Services/PlayerQueryService.cs\nsrc/BrazilianSoccerMcp/Tools/SoccerTools.cs\ntests/BrazilianSoccerMcp.Tests/BrazilianSoccerMcp.Tests.csproj\ntests/BrazilianSoccerMcp.Tests/TestData.cs  (shared fixture)\ntests/BrazilianSoccerMcp.Tests/CsvParserTests.cs\ntests/BrazilianSoccerMcp.Tests/TeamNameNormalizerTests.cs\ntests/BrazilianSoccerMcp.Tests/MatchQueryFeatureTests.cs\ntests/BrazilianSoccerMcp.Tests/TeamQueryFeatureTests.cs\ntests/BrazilianSoccerMcp.Tests/PlayerQueryFeatureTests.cs\ntests/BrazilianSoccerMcp.Tests/CompetitionQueryFeatureTests.cs\ntests/BrazilianSoccerMcp.Tests/StatisticsFeatureTests.cs\n```\n\ncsproj for the server:\n```xml\n<Project Sdk=\"Microsoft.NET.Sdk\">\n  <PropertyGroup>\n    <OutputType>Exe</OutputType>\n    <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>\n    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>\n    <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>\n    <InvariantGlobalization>false</InvariantGlobalization>\n  </PropertyGroup>\n  <ItemGroup>\n    <PackageReference Include=\"Microsoft.Extensions.Hosting\" Version=\"...\" />\n    <PackageReference Include=\"ModelContextProtocol\" Version=\"2.0.0\" />\n  </PropertyGroup>\n</Project>\n```\n\nModelContextProtocol 2.0.0 — need to check which TFM it targets (net8.0/net9.0 likely; net10.0 app can consume). Microsoft.Extensions.Hosting version — ModelContextProtocol depends on Microsoft.Extensions.Hosting.Abstractions; I'll add explicit Hosting 10.0.0? Hmm, mixing 10.0 extensions with MCP package should be fine. Actually ModelContextProtocol 2.0.0 might already depend on Microsoft.Extensions.Hosting (the meta-package) — the official README example for the stable package:\n\n```csharp\nvar builder = Host.CreateApplicationBuilder(args);\nbuilder.Logging.AddConsole(consoleLogOptions => { consoleLogOptions.LogToStandardErrorThreshold = LogLevel.Trace; });\nbuilder.Services.AddMcpServer().WithStdioServerTransport().WithToolsFromAssembly();\nawait builder.Build().RunAsync();\n```\n\nand csproj includes:\n```xml\n<PackageReference Include=\"Microsoft.Extensions.Hosting\" Version=\"9.0.x\" />\n<PackageReference Include=\"ModelContextProtocol\" Version=\"x\" />\n```\n\nI'll pin Microsoft.Extensions.Hosting to 10.0.0 (or let it resolve). Safer: add explicit reference with Version 10.0.0 — .NET 10 SDK, NuGet has it (GA since Nov 2025; today is Aug 2026). Fine.\n\nTest project: xunit 2.9.x + Microsoft.NET.Test.Sdk 17.x + xunit.runner.visualstudio. For .NET 10, test SDK 17.12+ or 18.x. I'll use latest stable versions and let restore resolve. xunit 2.9.3, Microsoft.NET.Test.Sdk 17.14.1, xunit.runner.visualstudio 3.1.x (runner vs 3.x works with xunit v2? xunit.runner.visualstudio 3.x supports both v2 and v3 test projects? Actually runner.visualstudio 3.x is for xunit v3; for xunit v2 use 2.8.x/3.x — 3.0 runner supports v2 tests too I believe. To be safe use xunit.runner.visualstudio 2.8.2 which pairs with xunit 2.9.x. Hmm, but does it support net10.0? Test discovery is TFM-agnostic mostly; Microsoft.NET.Test.Sdk 17.13+ supports .NET 10. I'll go xunit 2.9.3 + runner.visualstudio 3.1.4 + test sdk 17.14.1 — runner 3.x supports xunit.net v2 test assemblies (yes — the 3.x runner supports both v2 and v3). \n\nActually, to minimize risk, I could just use `dotnet test` with whatever resolves. Let me not overthink — if issues arise, adjust.\n\nNow the MCP tool API in ModelContextProtocol 2.0.0:\n- `[McpServerToolType]` on class, `[McpServerTool(Name = \"...\", Title=..., UseStructuredContent=...)]` on methods, `[Description(\"...\")]` on method and params from System.ComponentModel.\n- DI: tool classes can be non-static and get created via DI if registered? In the current SDK, `WithToolsFromAssembly()` scans for `[McpServerToolType]` classes; instance classes are created per-request via `ActivatorUtilities` against the request services — dependencies injected from DI work if registered. I'll register SoccerDataStore + services as singletons, and register the tool class too (safe).\n\nReturn type: string is fine (becomes text content).\n\nTool list design (10 tools):\n\n1. `list_competitions()` → text listing competitions with seasons and match counts (deduped by primary source? No — show raw per source or merged?). Show canonical competition groups with total unique seasons and match counts from primary sources. Simpler: list distinct competition keys with season ranges and counts from ALL rows. Fine for browsing.\n\n2. `find_matches(team?, opponent?, competition?, season?, date_from?, date_to?, venue?(\"home\"|\"away\"|\"any\"), round_or_stage?, limit=20)` — filters; \"final\" special for round_or_stage; sorts by date desc; returns formatted lines.\n\n3. `head_to_head(team, other_team, competition?)` — all matches + W/D/L summary.\n\n4. `team_record(team, season?, competition?, venue?)` — matches, W/D/L, GF/GA, win rate; by venue if given.\n\n5. `team_competitions(team)` — competitions + seasons the team appears in.\n\n6. `competition_standings(competition, season)` — table computed from primary source; marks champion and relegation (bottom 4) for 20-team leagues.\n\n7. `goal_statistics(competition?, season?)` — matches count, avg goals, home win %, draw %, away win %, avg home/away goals. Uses primary-source dedup when a specific competition+season given; for broad queries (all data) just aggregates raw (or deduped?) — dedup via primary source selection per (comp, season) group. I'll implement dedup selection generally: `GetPrimaryMatches()` groups by (CompetitionKey, Season) and picks best source group; returns union. Use that for standings + season stats + biggest wins. For raw browsing (find_matches), show all sources but tag with source? Showing duplicates would confuse. Hmm.\n\nDecision: by default, ALL match query tools operate on the **primary-source view** (deduped). The datastore keeps raw matches too. Display includes competition + season + round, which disambiguates. I'll note in tool description that duplicates across source files are collapsed.\n\nWait, but \"cross-file queries work (e.g. player + match data)\" — cross-file means combining player and match data, not showing duplicate match sources. Dedup is the right call.\n\n8. `biggest_wins(competition?, season?, limit=10)` — by goal margin, then total goals.\n\n9. `search_players(name?, nationality?, club?, position?, min_overall?, max_results=15)` — sorted by overall desc; position supports group names (\"forward\", \"midfielder\", \"defender\", \"goalkeeper\") or exact codes.\n\n10. `get_player(name)` — best match; details incl. attributes; suggestions when no exact.\n\nAlso maybe `compare_teams` = head_to_head covers it (\"Compare Palmeiras and Santos head-to-head\" ✓).\n\nFormatting helpers: format match line \"2023-09-03: Flamengo 2-1 Fluminense (Brasileirão Série A 2023, Round 22)\".\n\nNow normalization edge: Flamengo display names \"Flamengo-RJ\" → display base? For output I'll show raw team names as in data. Fine.\n\n**Date filtering**: parameters as strings \"2023-01-01\" parsed flexibly.\n\n**Season**: int? parameter; MCP schema — use string? Keep int? (nullable int) — MCP SDK handles int? fine. But \"NA\" seasons exist; queries just won't match.\n\nNow the implementation of primary-source selection:\n\n```csharp\npublic IReadOnlyList<MatchRecord> GetPrimaryMatches()\n{\n    // group by (CompetitionKey, Season) — season nullable → use -1 for null\n    return _matches\n        .GroupBy(m => (m.CompetitionKey, m.Season ?? -1))\n        .SelectMany(g => g.GroupBy(m => m.SourceFile)\n                          .OrderBy(s => SourceRank(s.Key))\n                          .First())\n        .ToList();\n}\n```\n\nSourceRank: Brasileirao_Matches.csv=0, Brazilian_Cup_Matches.csv=0, Libertadores_Matches.csv=0, novo_campeonato_brasileiro.csv=1, BR-Football-Dataset.csv=2. But careful: for Serie A 2015, historical (rank 1) has 380 and Brasileirao (rank 0) has 380 → picks Brasileirao ✓. For 2003-2011, only historical has data ✓. For 2023 Serie A, only BR-Football ✓. For Copa do Brasil 2022/2023, cup file ends 2021 → BR-Football ✓. \n\nEdge: BR-Football Serie A 2014 has 281 matches (partial), historical 2014 has 380, Brasileirao_Matches 380 → rank picks Brasileirao_Matches ✓.\n\n**Standings**: points 3/1/0; tiebreak: wins, GD, GF. Champion mark when the league looks complete (each team ≥ 30 matches?) — just mark top team as \"Champion\" and bottom 4 as \"Relegated\" for 20-team double round-robin (38 rounds). Only for brasileirao-serie-a with 20 teams. I'll compute and annotate; if teams != 20, skip annotations.\n\nSanity check expected: 2019 via primary source = Brasileirao_Matches 2019 → Flamengo champion with 90 pts (28W 6D 4L)? Real 2019: Flamengo 90 pts ✓. Great test.\n\n2020 relegation from data: bottom 4 in 2020 → real life: Vasco, Goiás, Coritiba, Botafogo. Let me not hardcode; test computes and checks count=4 and that all have fewest points.\n\n**Corinthians home record 2022** — spec example says 19 matches, 11W 5D 3L? That was the spec's illustrative example (maybe fictional). Test should compute from data and just assert 19 home matches and W+D+L=19. Let me verify actual numbers with python later for test expectations — better to write tests that assert structural invariants + a few verified facts (Flamengo 90 pts 2019; Neymar top Brazilian 92; 827 Brazilians; 380 matches per Serie A season 2012-2022).\n\nLet me verify Corinthians 2022 home and Fla-Flu count + 2023 Palmeiras matches (BR-Football 2023 team names \"Palmeiras\"?) with python now, so tests assert real numbers.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_fc2a6d9c8001EWK3uxBwpTwPN7","sessionID":"ses_03d6082b8ffevm2f5lm