{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1785677636572,"sessionID":"ses_03d6082b8ffevm2f5lmWdFLskw","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"todowrite","callID":"todowrite_22","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"todos":[{"content":"Verify concrete data facts for test expectations","status":"in_progress","priority":"high"},{"content":"Scaffold solution: server + test projects","status":"pending","priority":"high"},{"content":"Implement data layer (CSV parser, normalizers, data store, models)","status":"pending","priority":"high"},{"content":"Implement query services (matches, players)","status":"pending","priority":"high"},{"content":"Implement MCP tools + Program.cs (stdio server)","status":"pending","priority":"high"},{"content":"Write BDD-style xUnit tests (20+ scenarios)","status":"pending","priority":"high"},{"content":"Build, run tests, verify MCP server starts","status":"pending","priority":"high"}]},"output":"[\n  {\n    \"content\": \"Verify concrete data facts for test expectations\",\n    \"status\": \"in_progress\",\n    \"priority\": \"high\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"content\": \"Scaffold solution: server + test projects\",\n    \"status\": \"pending\",\n    \"priority\": \"high\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"content\": \"Implement data layer (CSV parser, normalizers, data store, models)\",\n    \"status\": \"pending\",\n    \"priority\": \"high\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"content\": \"Implement query services (matches, players)\",\n    \"status\": \"pending\",\n    \"priority\": \"high\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"content\": \"Implement MCP tools + Program.cs (stdio server)\",\n    \"status\": \"pending\",\n    \"priority\": \"high\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"content\": \"Write BDD-style xUnit tests (20+ scenarios)\",\n    \"status\": \"pending\",\n    \"priority\": \"high\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"content\": \"Build, run tests, verify MCP server starts\",\n    \"status\": \"pending\",\n    \"priority\": \"high\"\n  }\n]","metadata":{"todos":[{"content":"Verify concrete data facts for test expectations","status":"in_progress","priority":"high"},{"content":"Scaffold solution: server + test projects","status":"pending","priority":"high"},{"content":"Implement data layer (CSV parser, normalizers, data store, models)","status":"pending","priority":"high"},{"content":"Implement query services (matches, players)","status":"pending","priority":"high"},{"content":"Implement MCP tools + Program.cs (stdio server)","status":"pending","priority":"high"},{"content":"Write BDD-style xUnit tests (20+ scenarios)","status":"pending","priority":"high"},{"content":"Build, run tests, verify MCP server starts","status":"pending","priority":"high"}],"truncated":false},"title":"7 todos","time":{"start":1785677636552,"end":1785677636554}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"Verified facts for tests:\n- 2019 standings: Flamengo 90 pts (28W 6D 4L), Santos 74, Palmeiras 74 ✓ (matches spec example exactly!)\n- 2019 relegated: Cruzeiro, CSA, Chapecoense, Avaí ✓\n- Corinthians home 2022 (played matches in dataset): 15 played, 10W 4D 1L, GF 21 GA 7. Note: 4 of 19 home matches have NA goals (2022 season data may be truncated). So test: home matches 2022 total = 19, played = 15, W=10 D=4 L=1. Hmm wait — spec says W11 D5 L3 with 19 matches... that spec example was aspirational/illustrative; actual data says 10/4/1 of 15 played. My tests assert the ACTUAL data: 19 home matches, 15 with recorded scores, 10 wins. I'll assert played record sums correctly.\n\nHmm, actually wait. 82 NA rows of 4180 — which seasons? Probably 2022 (dataset snapshot mid-season?). Let me check per season... 2022: 380 - played? Let me quickly count. Not critical: stats tools will use played matches only, and mention unplayed count.\n\n- Fla-Flu played in Brasileirao_Matches: 22 ✓ (2012-2022, 11 seasons × 2 = 22 ✓ complete)\n\nNow — implementation time. Let me create the todo list and start writing code.\n\nFiles plan (final):\n\n**src/BrazilianSoccerMcp/BrazilianSoccerMcp.csproj**\n**src/BrazilianSoccerMcp/Program.cs** — host + stdio + DI\n**src/BrazilianSoccerMcp/Data/CsvParser.cs** — streaming CSV parser (quotes, embedded commas/newlines)\n**src/BrazilianSoccerMcp/Data/DataPathResolver.cs** — find data/kaggle (env override BRAZILIAN_SOCCER_DATA_DIR, else walk up from cwd/assembly)\n**src/BrazilianSoccerMcp/Data/TextNormalizer.cs** — diacritics removal + compact key\n**src/BrazilianSoccerMcp/Data/TeamNameNormalizer.cs** — alias table + state suffix\n**src/BrazilianSoccerMcp/Models/MatchRecord.cs**\n**src/BrazilianSoccerMcp/Models/PlayerRecord.cs**\n**src/BrazilianSoccerMcp/Data/SoccerDataStore.cs** — loads all 6 CSVs; exposes Matches (raw), PrimaryMatches (deduped), Players\n**src/BrazilianSoccerMcp/Services/MatchQueryService.cs** — find, h2h, record, standings, stats, biggest wins, competitions-of-team\n**src/BrazilianSoccerMcp/Services/PlayerQueryService.cs** — search, get by name\n**src/BrazilianSoccerMcp/Tools/SoccerTools.cs** — 10 MCP tools returning formatted text\n\n**tests/BrazilianSoccerMcp.Tests/...** as planned.\n\nKey design decisions:\n\n1. MatchRecord:\n```csharp\npublic sealed record MatchRecord\n{\n    public required string Competition { get; init; }       // display\n    public required string CompetitionKey { get; init; }    // normalized\n    public DateTime? Date { get; init; }\n    public required string HomeTeam { get; init; }  // raw display\n    public required string AwayTeam { get; init; }\n    public required string HomeKey { get; init; }\n    public required string AwayKey { get; init; }\n    public int? HomeGoals { get; init; }\n    public int? AwayGoals { get; init; }\n    public int? Season { get; init; }\n    public string? RoundOrStage { get; init; }\n    public string? Venue { get; init; }   // arena when known\n    public required string SourceFile { get; init; }\n    public bool Played => HomeGoals.HasValue && AwayGoals.HasValue;\n    // optional extended stats (BR-Football)\n    public int? HomeShots { get; init; } ...\n}\n```\nSkip extended stats fields to keep simple? The spec lists corners/shots/attacks columns. \"Must use the provided datasets\" — loading is enough; but exposing corner stats could be nice. Keep minimal: I'll include HomeCorners/AwayCorners/HomeShots/AwayShots nullable — cheap to add, and find_matches detail could mention them. Actually keep it lean: skip extended stats in output; include them in the model only if used. Decision: skip. Keep the model minimal (YAGNI) — but data coverage criterion says \"All 6 CSV files are loadable and queryable\" — BR-Football matches ARE queryable (that's the file's role). OK.\n\n2. DateOnly vs DateTime: use DateTime? with date part; format \"yyyy-MM-dd\".\n\n3. Primary-source dedup: group (CompetitionKey, Season ?? -1) → pick best SourceRank group. Rank map by source file name.\n\n4. Standings calculator: from played matches of a competition+season (primary view). Sort: pts desc, wins desc, GD desc, GF desc, name.\n\n5. Team record: overall/home/away; uses primary view.\n\n6. Player search: name substring (normalized, diacritic-free), nationality exact-ish (normalized equality or substring), club normalized-key equality OR substring on display, position exact code or group, min_overall. Sort overall desc, then name. Limit.\n\n7. get_player: exact normalized name match → substring contains → suggestions (top by overall among contains on last name?). Return formatted details.\n\n8. MCP tools — attributes: [McpServerToolType], [McpServerTool], [Description]. In ModelContextProtocol 2.0.0, `McpServerToolAttribute` has properties Name, Title, etc. Description via [Description]. Fine.\n\nDI: \n```csharp\nbuilder.Services.AddSingleton(sp => SoccerDataStore.Load(DataPathResolver.Resolve(...)));\nbuilder.Services.AddSingleton<MatchQueryService>();\nbuilder.Services.AddSingleton<PlayerQueryService>();\nbuilder.Services.AddMcpServer().WithStdioServerTransport().WithToolsFromAssembly();\n```\nWithToolsFromAssembly picks up [McpServerToolType] classes; instance tools are created via ActivatorUtilities per invocation — constructor deps from the request service provider, singletons flow. \n\nLogging to stderr to keep stdout clean for JSON-RPC: `builder.Logging.AddConsole(o => o.LogToStandardErrorThreshold = LogLevel.Trace)` — hmm, simpler: `builder.Logging.SetMinimumLevel(LogLevel.Warning)`? Default Host console logging writes to stdout which CORRUPTS the stdio transport? Actually Host.CreateApplicationBuilder doesn't add console logging by default... it does add Console logger via CreateApplicationBuilder defaults? Host.CreateApplicationBuilder adds console logging providers. The MCP docs example explicitly redirects to stderr. I'll follow the canonical example.\n\nData path resolution for the SERVER: cwd when run via `dotnet run` from repo root works; when run as MCP server by a client, cwd may be anywhere. Strategy:\n1. env var `BRAZILIAN_SOCCER_DATA_DIR` (or first CLI arg `--data-dir <path>`)\n2. walk up from AppContext.BaseDirectory looking for `data/kaggle`\n3. walk up from Directory.GetCurrentDirectory()\n4. error with clear message.\n\nTests: resolve by walking up from test assembly base dir — repo has data/kaggle at root ✓.\n\n9. CSV parser: handle quotes, escaped quotes (\"\"), embedded newlines. Simple state machine yielding string[] rows. Skip empty lines. Tolerant: if a row has fewer fields than header, pad with nulls.\n\nFIFA CSV: 88 columns, first col is unnamed index. DictReader-like helper: build header index map; helper Get(row, \"Name\").\n\n10. Encoding: UTF-8 (files verified). Historical file might have BOM — StreamReader handles BOM automatically.\n\nNow about nullable Season for Libertadores 'NA' rows: parse season with int.TryParse → null. Date parse: multiple formats. Libertadores 'NA' season rows — what do their dates look like? Probably still dated. Fine.\n\nPerformance: all in-memory, queries are LINQ over ≤ 18k rows → ms. \n\nLet me also double-check the cup \"final\" handling: tool find_matches(round_or_stage: \"final\") — for cup (numeric rounds), resolve: max round per season (after other filters). Implement: if round_or_stage equals \"final\" (case-insensitive), then keep matches whose RoundOrStage equals the max numeric round for their (competition, season) group within the filtered set, OR whose RoundOrStage contains \"final\" (Libertadores stage 'final' ✓). \n\nImplementation detail: RoundOrStage for cup = round number as string (\"1\"..\"8\"); for Brasileirão = round number; for Libertadores = stage text. For display \"Round 22\" vs \"group stage\". I'll store RoundOrStage raw and format by source type... simpler: display \"round/stage: X\"? The spec example output shows \"(Brasileirão Round 22)\". I'll store `Round` (int?) and `Stage` (string?) separately. Display: if Stage present → Stage; elif Round → $\"Round {Round}\". Finals: cup final = max Round in season; Libertadores final = Stage==\"final\".\n\nFind matches between teams: exact normalized keys. Substring fallback when exact key not found among known teams: pick candidate teams whose display-normalized contains query or vice versa, use their keys (all of them). This helps \"Vasco\" vs \"Vasco da Gama\" (alias handles anyway) and \"United\" type junk. Implement `ResolveTeamKeys(query)`:\n- key = normalizer.Normalize(query)\n- if key in knownKeys → {key}\n- else candidates = knownKeys where display contains normalizedQuery or key contains queryKey (on compact keys) → those keys (cap at ~10)\n- if still none → empty (tool responds \"team not found, did you mean...\" with closest matches by substring on raw names... maybe just list a few alphabetically-close? Keep simple: suggest teams whose compact key starts with first 4 chars of query). Nice-to-have; implement basic suggestion via substring.\n\nKnownKeys: distinct HomeKey/AwayKey across matches + club keys from players (for club-centric queries). For match tools, use match keys.\n\nFor team display in outputs: prefer a canonical display name: most frequent raw name for that key in the relevant match set? Use most frequent raw across all matches, stripping the state suffix? Eh — display raw names as they appear per match is fine and truthful; for team headers use the most common raw name with suffix stripped... Keep: canonical display = most frequent raw form; in match lines use raw per-match names.\n\nHmm, \"Flamengo-RJ\" in match lines looks technical but honest. The spec's own examples show \"Flamengo 2-1 Fluminense\". I'll strip state suffix for display: helper `DisplayName(raw)` = paren-strip + state-suffix strip + trim (keeps accents). \"Flamengo-RJ\"→\"Flamengo\", \"América - MG\"→\"América\", \"Botafogo RJ\"→\"Botafogo RJ\" hmm BR-Football uses space-separated state \"Botafogo RJ\" — my state regex should also handle trailing \" RJ\" without dash when the word is a state code AND preceded by a space... \"Vasco Da Gama RJ\" → \"Vasco Da Gama\". But careful: \"Athletico Paranaense\" — \"Paranaense\" isn't a state code ✓. \"America MG\" → \"America\". \"Fortaleza FC\"? \"FC\" not a state ✓ stays. \"EC Bahia\" stays. OK: regex `\\s*-?\\s*(AC|AL|AP|AM|BA|CE|DF|ES|GO|MA|MT|MS|MG|PA|PB|PR|PE|PI|RJ|RN|RS|RO|RR|SC|SP|SE|TO)$` case-insensitive on display names. But \"Bahia\" ends with... no. Wait — risk: team names ending in two letters that form a state code without separator, e.g. \"Csa-AL\"→\"Csa\" ✓ dash present; \"Fortaleza\" — ends \"za\" not a state ✓. The regex requires either dash or space before the 2 letters: `[\\s-]+(XX)$` — \"Botafogo RJ\" ✓, \"America MG\" ✓, \"Csa-AL\" ✓. A name like \"Real Noroeste Capixaba\"? no trailing state. \"União\"? ends \"ao\" — ão isn't state. OK but note \"TO\" — \"Toronto\"? not in data. Fine.\n\nFor the compact key (matching), I remove ALL non-letters/digits then strip trailing state code — \"botafogorj\"→\"botafogo\" ✓ (after alias check). But danger: names genuinely ending in a state-code substring without separator: \"santos\" no; \"goias\" ends \"as\"? not a state; \"ceara\" ends \"ra\"? not a state; \"avas\"? Hmm \"Avaí\"→\"avai\" ends \"ai\" not state. \"Paraná\"→\"parana\" ends \"na\" no. \"Vasco\" no. Wait — what about \"Sport\"? no. \"Inter de Limeira\"? ends \"ra\". \"São Raimundo\"? \"do\". Hmm \"Maranhão\"→\"maranhao\" ends \"ao\" — not a state code ✓ (\"MA\" is, \"ao\" no). \"Tocantinópolis\"? \"is\". What ends with a real code falsely? \"Paysandu\" \"du\" no. \"Remo\" \"mo\" no. \"Treze\" \"ze\" no. \"Náutico\"→\"nautico\" \"co\" no. \"Santa Cruz\"→\"santacruz\" \"uz\" no. \"Confiança\"→\"confianca\" \"ca\" — CA is NOT a Brazilian state (states: AC AL AP AM BA CE DF ES GO MA MT MS MG PA PB PR PE PI RJ RN RS RO RR SC SP SE TO) — \"ca\" not in list ✓. \"CRB\"→\"crb\" \"rb\" no. \"ASA\"→\"asa\" \"sa\" no. \"ABC\"→\"abc\" \"bc\" no. \"Operário\"? \"io\" no. What about \"CE\"? \"Vocem\"? \"em\" no. Names ending \"se\": SE is Sergipe! \"Paranaense\"→\"paranaense\" ends \"se\"!!! That would strip to \"paranaen\". DANGER. \n\nSo blind trailing-2-letter strip on compact keys is WRONG (\"paranaense\"→ strips \"se\" ✗). Mitigation: only strip state suffix from the compact key when the ORIGINAL string had a separator (dash or space) before the state code. I.e., do state stripping on the spaced/normalized string with the separator regex FIRST (like display), THEN compact. \"Athletico Paranaense\" — no trailing state token (last word \"Paranaense\" not a code) → key \"athleticoparanaense\" ✓. \"Athletico-PR\" → strip → \"athletico\" → but alias-first handles \"athletico-pr\"→\"athleticoparanaense\" ✓ (alias keys on compacted-with-separator form? Let me define carefully):\n\nNormalizer algorithm (final):\n```\nstring Normalize(string? raw):\n  if empty → \"\"\n  s = raw.Trim()\n  s = Regex.Replace(s, @\"\\s*\\([^)]*\\)\", \" \")     // drop parentheticals\n  s = RemoveDiacritics(s).ToLowerInvariant()\n  s = s.Replace('–','-').Replace('—','-')\n  // alias pass on full compact form incl. state\n  full = Compact(s)                                // keep only letters/digits\n  if Aliases.TryGetValue(full, out var a) return a;\n  // strip trailing state token when separated by space/dash\n  s2 = Regex.Replace(s, @\"[\\s\\-]+\\(?([A-Z]{2})\\)?\\s*$\", \"\", IgnoreCase) when group in States\n  stripped = Compact(s2)\n  if (stripped != full && Aliases.TryGetValue(stripped, out var a2)) return a2;\n  return stripped.Length > 0 ? stripped : full;\n```\nAliases (compact full → canonical):\n- \"athleticopr\"→\"athleticoparanaense\", \"atleticopr\"→same, \"atleticoparanaense\"→same\n- \"atleticogo\"→\"atleticogoianiense\", \"athleticogo\"→same\n- \"atleticomg\"→\"atleticomineiro\"\n- \"americamg\"→\"americamineiro\", \"americafc\"→\"americamineiro\", \"america\"→\"americamineiro\"  (careful: \"america\" alias also catches \"América - MG\" ✓ and any \"America\"-only; América-RN would strip to \"america\"→mineiro — wrong for RN but América-RN is minor; accept, note in comment. Hmm — \"America - RN\"? does the data have it? Cup data has \"América - MG\". Historical has \"América\"? Let me not sweat.)\n- \"sport\"→\"sportrecife\", \"sportclubdorecife\"→\"sportrecife\", \"sportpe\"→\"sportrecife\"\n- \"vasco\"→\"vascodagama\", \"vascodagamarj\"→\"vascodagama\"\n- \"botafogorj\"→\"botafogo\", \"botafogosp\"→\"botafogosp\"?? — wait: with my algorithm \"botafogosp\" alias → \"botafogosp\" returns distinct key; but I need entries so that RJ→\"botafogo\" and SP/PB stay distinct: alias \"botafogorj\"→\"botafogo\"; \"botafogosp\"→\"botafogosp\" (self, distinct); \"botafogopb\"→\"botafogopb\". But FIFA \"Botafogo\" → key \"botafogo\" ✓ (FIFA Botafogo is the RJ club ✓).\n  Also \"americarn\"? not alias → strip → \"america\" → alias → \"americamineiro\" ✗ wrong but obscure; add alias \"americarn\"→\"americarn\" (self) to protect. Also \"americape\"? \"americato\"? Add protective self-aliases for america variants: rn, pe... I'll add \"americarn\" self-alias only (that's the other América in Brazilian football). \n- \"cearasportingclub\"→\"ceara\"\n- \"ecbahia\"→\"bahia\", \"esporteclubebahia\"→\"bahia\"\n- \"fortalezafc\"→\"fortaleza\", \"fortalezaesporteclube\"→\"fortaleza\"\n- \"saopaulofc\"? FIFA has no São Paulo. skip.\n- \"gremiofbpa\"? not in data. skip.\n- \"inter\" → hmm, \"Internacional\" vs \"Inter de Limeira\" vs Inter Milan (Libertadores? no). FIFA Club \"Inter\" = Inter Milan (Gabriel Barbosa's club — but he's not in data). Don't alias \"inter\".\n- \"athletico\"→\"athleticoparanaense\"? \"Athletico-PR\" is alias-first handled; bare \"athletico\" arises only from... nothing after alias-first. Skip.\n- \"parana\"→\"parana\" ✓ natural.\n- \"ponte preta\" etc fine.\n- \"santos\" — FIFA \"Santos\" (Brazilian club, 20 players) vs Mexican \"Santos Laguna\" in Libertadores → keys \"santos\" vs \"santoslaguna\" distinct ✓ good.\n- \"boavista\" — \"Boavista Sport Club (antigo...)\" → strip paren → \"boavista sport club\" → compact \"boavistasportclub\"; FIFA \"Boavista FC\" (Portugal) → \"boavistafc\". Different clubs, keep distinct, no alias ✓. Query \"Boavista\" → key \"boavista\" — matches neither exactly! ResolveTeamKeys substring fallback handles it (candidates both). Good demonstration of fallback.\n\nThat's robust enough. Also \"guarani\" vs \"Guarani de Palhoça\"? fine.\n\nLibertadores foreign teams: \"Barcelona-EQU\" → state strip? \"EQU\" is 3 letters — not stripped (states are 2) → key \"barcelonaequ\" ✓ distinct. \"Nacional (URU)\" → paren-strip → \"nacional\" — \"Nacional-PB\"? Brazilian Nacional? rare. fine. \"River Plate\" → \"riverplate\". \"River-PAR\"? \"par\" 3 letters kept. OK.\n\n11. Now the test count: I promised ≥ 20 sample questions — I'll write ~30 focused tests across feature files.\n\nAlso need: solution file. `dotnet new sln`, `dotnet new console`, `dotnet new xunit`.\n\nLet me now create everything. First the todos, then scaffold via dotnet CLI, then write files.\n\nOne more decision — project name/namespace: `BrazilianSoccerMcp`. RootNamespace default fine.\n\nMCP package: ModelContextProtocol 2.0.0 stable. Microsoft.Extensions.Hosting 10.0.0.\n\nTool methods return string. Parameter descriptions via [Description]. Nullable optional params with defaults — MCP SDK supports optional parameters with default values ✓.\n\nDate parsing helper: formats [\"yyyy-MM-dd HH:mm:ss\", \"yyyy-MM-dd\", \"dd/MM/yyyy\", \"yyyy-MM-ddTHH:mm:ss\", \"dd/MM/yyyy HH:mm\"] invariant.\n\nGoal parsing: \"2\", \"2.0\", \"NA\", \"\" → int? via double.TryParse.\n\nSeason parsing: \"2012\" int? ; \"NA\" → null.\n\nNow — writing the code. Let me be careful with the ModelContextProtocol 2.0.0 API. Key types:\n- `ModelContextProtocol.Server.McpServerToolTypeAttribute`\n- `ModelContextProtocol.Server.McpServerToolAttribute`\n- `System.ComponentModel.DescriptionAttribute`\n- Host ext: `Microsoft.Extensions.Hosting.HostingHostBuilderExtensions`? The MCP extensions: `builder.Services.AddMcpServer()` in namespace `Microsoft.Extensions.DependencyInjection`? It's in `ModelContextProtocol` (Microsoft.Extensions.AI? no). The canonical README:\n\n```csharp\nusing Microsoft.Extensions.DependencyInjection;\nusing Microsoft.Extensions.Hosting;\nusing ModelContextProtocol.Server;\nusing System.ComponentModel;\n\nvar builder = Host.CreateApplicationBuilder(args);\nbuilder.Logging.AddConsole(consoleLogOptions =>\n{\n    consoleLogOptions.LogToStandardErrorThreshold = LogLevel.Trace;\n});\nbuilder.Services\n    .AddMcpServer()\n    .WithStdioServerTransport()\n    .WithToolsFromAssembly();\nawait builder.Build().RunAsync();\n\n[McpServerToolType]\npublic static class EchoTool\n{\n    [McpServerTool, Description(\"Echoes the message back to the client.\")]\n    public static string Echo([Description(\"The message to echo back\")] string message) => $\"hello {message}\";\n}\n```\n\n`LogToStandardErrorThreshold` lives in `Microsoft.Extensions.Logging.Console` — available via Microsoft.Extensions.Hosting's transitive Console logging package ✓.\n\nFor instance tool classes with DI: supported — \"Tools can be static or instance; instance tool types are resolved from the service provider (ActivatorUtilities)\". I'll register the tool class in DI to be explicit: `builder.Services.AddSingleton<SoccerTools>();` — WithToolsFromAssembly will find it via assembly scan and resolve from DI ✓.\n\nAlso `McpServerToolAttribute.Name` property to set snake_case names: `[McpServerTool(Name = \"find_matches\")]`. By default the tool name is the method name — C# method names are PascalCase. I'll set explicit names.\n\nReadOnlySpan? no. Let me write everything now.\n\nTest project packages: Microsoft.NET.Test.Sdk 17.14.1, xunit 2.9.3, xunit.runner.visualstudio 3.1.4 (supports v2 tests), coverlet? skip collector to keep lean — include coverlet.collector 6.0.4? Not necessary; skip.\n\nLet me also plan the exact public API surface used by tests (so tests compile against services directly, not MCP):\n\nSoccerDataStore:\n- static SoccerDataStore Load(string dataDir)\n- IReadOnlyList<MatchRecord> Matches (all sources)\n- IReadOnlyList<MatchRecord> PrimaryMatches (deduped primary view)\n- IReadOnlyList<PlayerRecord> Players\n- IReadOnlyDictionary<string,string> TeamDisplayNames (key → canonical display)\n- static readonly string[] RequiredFiles? internal.\n\nMatchQueryService(dataStore):\n- IReadOnlyList<MatchRecord> FindMatches(MatchQuery q) — q record with Team?, Opponent?, Competition?, Season?, DateFrom?, DateTo?, Venue (enum any/home/away), Round?, Stage?/FinalsOnly, Limit\n- HeadToHeadResult HeadToHead(string teamA, string teamB, string? competition=null)\n- TeamRecordResult TeamRecord(string team, int? season=null, string? competition=null, Venue venue=Any)\n- IReadOnlyList<StandingsRow> Standings(string competition, int season, out string? note)\n- GoalStatsResult GoalStatistics(string? competition, int? season)\n- IReadOnlyList<MatchRecord> BiggestWins(string? competition, int? season, int limit)\n- IReadOnlyList<CompetitionSummary> ListCompetitions()\n- IReadOnlyList<TeamCompetitionInfo> TeamCompetitions(string team)\n- IReadOnlyList<string> ResolveTeamKeys(string query, out bool exact) — internal? make public for tests.\n\nPlayerQueryService(dataStore):\n- PlayerSearchResult Search(PlayerQuery q)\n- PlayerRecord? FindByName(string name, out IReadOnlyList<PlayerRecord> suggestions)\n\nTools format with StringBuilder.\n\nNumber formatting: win rate \"57.9%\" → invariant culture 1 decimal.\n\nLet me now write all files. I'll create the directory structure with dotnet new.\n\nAlso a global.json? No — SDK 10 installed, fine.\n\nOne more consideration: `dotnet new xunit` with net10.0 — template version may default to xunit v3? `dotnet new xunit` in SDK 10 creates an xunit v3 project (\"xunit\" template → xunit.v3? In recent SDKs, `dotnet new xunit` uses xunit.v3 1.x/2.x with Microsoft.Testing.Platform). That changes `dotnet test` behavior (MTP runner). xunit v3 works with `dotnet test` too. Either is fine — I'll create with the template and inspect. Actually simpler: write csproj files by hand with pinned packages; avoids template surprises. I'll hand-write both csproj and all sources, then `dotnet restore/build/test`.\n\nTest csproj:\n```xml\n<Project Sdk=\"Microsoft.NET.Sdk\">\n  <PropertyGroup>\n    <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>\n    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>\n    <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>\n    <IsPackable>false</IsPackable>\n  </PropertyGroup>\n  <ItemGroup>\n    <PackageReference Include=\"Microsoft.NET.Test.Sdk\" Version=\"17.14.1\" />\n    <PackageReference Include=\"xunit\" Version=\"2.9.3\" />\n    <PackageReference Include=\"xunit.runner.visualstudio\" Version=\"3.1.4\">\n      <PrivateAssets>all</PrivateAssets>\n      <IncludeAssets>runtime; build; native; contentfiles; analyzers; buildtransitive</IncludeAssets>\n    </PackageReference>\n  </ItemGroup>\n  <ItemGroup>\n    <ProjectReference Include=\"..\\..\\src\\BrazilianSoccerMcp\\BrazilianSoccerMcp.csproj\" />\n  </ItemGroup>\n</Project>\n```\n\nServer csproj:\n```xml\n<Project Sdk=\"Microsoft.NET.Sdk\">\n  <PropertyGroup>\n    <OutputType>Exe</OutputType>\n    <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>\n    <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>\n    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>\n    <RootNamespace>BrazilianSoccerMcp</RootNamespace>\n    <AssemblyName>brazilian-soccer-mcp</AssemblyName>\n  </PropertyGroup>\n  <ItemGroup>\n    <PackageReference Include=\"Microsoft.Extensions.Hosting\" Version=\"10.0.0\" />\n    <PackageReference Include=\"ModelContextProtocol\" Version=\"2.0.0\" />\n  </ItemGroup>\n</Project>\n```\n\nAssemblyName with dashes → RootNamespace stays valid C#. Assembly name doesn't need to be a valid identifier. OK.\n\nNow write all source files. Let me draft each carefully.\n\n### TextNormalizer.cs\n```csharp\nusing System.Globalization;\nusing System.Text;\n\nnamespace BrazilianSoccerMcp.Data;\n\n/// <summary>Diacritic-insensitive text normalization helpers.</summary>\npublic static class TextNormalizer\n{\n    public static string RemoveDiacritics(string value)\n    {\n        var decomposed = value.Normalize(NormalizationForm.FormD);\n        var sb = new StringBuilder(decomposed.Length);\n        foreach (var c in decomposed)\n        {\n            if (CharUnicodeInfo.GetUnicodeCategory(c) != UnicodeCategory.NonSpacingMark)\n                sb.Append(c);\n        }\n        return sb.ToString().Normalize(NormalizationForm.FormC);\n    }\n\n    /// <summary>Lowercase, diacritic-free, letters/digits only.</summary>\n    public static string CompactKey(string value)\n    {\n        var cleaned = RemoveDiacritics(value).ToLowerInvariant();\n        var sb = new StringBuilder(cleaned.Length);\n        foreach (var c in cleaned)\n        {\n            if (char.IsLetterOrDigit(c)) sb.Append(c);\n        }\n        return sb.ToString();\n    }\n}\n```\n\n### TeamNameNormalizer.cs\n```csharp\nusing System.Text.RegularExpressions;\n\nnamespace BrazilianSoccerMcp.Data;\n\npublic static class TeamNameNormalizer\n{\n    private static readonly HashSet<string> States = new(StringComparer.OrdinalIgnoreCase)\n    { \"AC\",\"AL\",\"AP\",\"AM\",\"BA\",\"CE\",\"DF\",\"ES\",\"GO\",\"MA\",\"MT\",\"MS\",\"MG\",\"PA\",\"PB\",\"PR\",\"PE\",\"PI\",\"RJ\",\"RN\",\"RS\",\"RO\",\"RR\",\"SC\",\"SP\",\"SE\",\"TO\" };\n\n    private static readonly Regex Parenthetical = new(@\"\\s*\\([^)]*\\)\", RegexOptions.Compiled);\n    private static readonly Regex TrailingState = new(@\"[\\s\\-]+([A-Za-z]{2})\\s*$\", RegexOptions.Compiled);\n\n    // canonical aliases on compact keys\n    private static readonly Dictionary<string, string> Aliases = new()\n    {\n        [\"athleticopr\"] = \"athleticoparanaense\",\n        [\"atleticopr\"] = \"athleticoparanaense\",\n        [\"atleticoparanaense\"] = \"athleticoparanaense\",\n        [\"atleticogo\"] = \"atleticogoianiense\",\n        [\"athleticogo\"] = \"atleticogoianiense\",\n        [\"atleticomg\"] = \"atleticomineiro\",\n        [\"americamg\"] = \"americamineiro\",\n        [\"americafc\"] = \"americamineiro\",\n        [\"america\"] = \"americamineiro\",\n        [\"americarn\"] = \"americarn\", // keep América de Natal distinct\n        [\"sport\"] = \"sportrecife\",\n        [\"sportpe\"] = \"sportrecife\",\n        [\"sportclubdorecife\"] = \"sportrecife\",\n        [\"vasco\"] = \"vascodagama\",\n        [\"vascodagamarj\"] = \"vascodagama\",\n        [\"botafogorj\"] = \"botafogo\",\n        [\"botafogosp\"] = \"botafogosp\", // distinct from Botafogo de Futebol e Regatas (RJ)\n        [\"botafogopb\"] = \"botafogopb\",\n        [\"cearasportingclub\"] = \"ceara\",\n        [\"ecbahia\"] = \"bahia\",\n        [\"esporteclubebahia\"] = \"bahia\",\n        [\"fortalezafc\"] = \"fortaleza\",\n        [\"fortalezaesporteclube\"] = \"fortaleza\",\n    };\n\n    public static string NormalizeKey(string? raw)\n    {\n        if (string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(raw)) return string.Empty;\n        var s = Parenthetical.Replace(raw.Trim(), \" \");\n        s = s.Replace('–', '-').Replace('—', '-');\n        var full = TextNormalizer.CompactKey(s);\n        if (Aliases.TryGetValue(full, out var alias)) return alias;\n\n        var m = TrailingState.Match(s);\n        string stripped = full;\n        if (m.Success && States.Contains(m.Groups[1].Value))\n        {\n            stripped = TextNormalizer.CompactKey(s[..m.Index]);\n            if (stripped.Length == 0) stripped = full;\n        }\n        if (stripped != full && Aliases.TryGetValue(stripped, out var alias2)) return alias2;\n        return stripped;\n    }\n\n    /// <summary>Pretty display: drop parentheticals and trailing state token, keep accents.</summary>\n    public static string DisplayName(string? raw)\n    {\n        if (string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(raw)) return raw ?? string.Empty;\n        var s = Parenthetical.Replace(raw.Trim(), \" \").Trim();\n        var m = TrailingState.Match(s);\n        if (m.Success && States.Contains(m.Groups[1].Value))\n            s = s[..m.Index].Trim();\n        return Regex.Replace(s, @\"\\s+\", \" \");\n    }\n}\n```\n\nWait, one problem: TrailingState regex requires separator `[\\s\\-]+` before 2 letters. \"Csa-AL\" → strips ✓ → \"Csa\". \"America MG\" ✓. \"Botafogo RJ\" ✓. But \"Flamengo-RJ\": strip → \"Flamengo\" ✓. \"Athletico-PR\": alias-first ✓. Good.\n\nEdge: \"Nacional (URU)\" → parenthetical removed → \"nacional\" ✓.\n\nEdge: compact of \"Boavista Sport Club (antigo Esporte Clube Barreira) - RJ\" → paren removed → \"Boavista Sport Club  - RJ\" → trailing state strips \"RJ\" (separator present: \" - RJ\") → \"boavistasportclub\" ✓.\n\n\"Barcelona-EQU\": TrailingState matches \"-EQ\"? Regex `[\\s\\-]+([A-Za-z]{2})\\s*$` on \"Barcelona-EQU\": it would match \"-EQ\" leaving \"U\"?? `([A-Za-z]{2})\\s*$` — the last two letters \"QU\" preceded by... Let me think: regex matches from right: separator chars then exactly 2 letters then end. \"Barcelona-EQU\": ends \"U\", two letters \"QU\", preceded by \"E\" — not separator. Alternative: letters \"EQ\" at positions... \"EQU\" — for the regex to match, we need sep + 2 letters + end. The string ends \"...-EQU\". Possibilities: sep at \"-\" then letters must be exactly 2 then end → \"-EQ\" then \"U\" remains → fail (letters must be followed by \\s*$). Regex backtracking: `[\\s\\-]+` could match \"-\", `([A-Za-z]{2})` = \"EQ\", then `\\s*$` fails due to \"U\". No other separator position (only one dash). So no match ✓ \"barcelonaequ\" stays ✓.\n\nBut what about \"River Plate\"? no trailing state ✓. \"Atletico Mineiro\"? \"ro\" not state ✓. \"Sao Paulo\"? \"lo\" ✓. \"Fortaleza FC\"? \"FC\" not a state ✓. \"EC Bahia\"? ends \"ia\" ✓. \"America MG\"→ strips to \"america\"→ alias ✓.\n\nOne more: \"Goias\"? \"as\" not state ✓. \"Gremio\" ✓. \"Cuiaba\" \"ba\"?? \"Cuiaba\" ends with \"ba\" but needs separator before — \"Cuiaba\" is one word, no separator → no strip ✓. The regex requires `[\\s\\-]+` before the two letters. \"Csa-AL\" dash ✓. OK — but what about compact-key safety: strip happens on spaced string THEN compact, so \"paranaense\" never risks losing \"se\" ✓ (no separator before \"se\" in \"Athletico Paranaense\" — wait, \"Paranaense\" ends \"se\" but the regex needs sep + exactly 2 letters + end; \"Paranaense\" = many letters; `([A-Za-z]{2})\\s*$` would need the last token to be exactly 2 letters. \"Paranaense\" is one 10-letter token; regex `[\\s\\-]+([A-Za-z]{2})$` — separator, then 2 letters, then end. In \"... Paranaense\", the only separators are spaces before \"Paranaense\"; after that space, `([A-Za-z]{2})\\s*$` must consume \"Paranaense\" (10 chars) with a 2-char class → fails. Backtracking finds no other separator. No match ✓.\n\nThe ONLY risk: team whose last token IS a 2-letter word that equals a state code. E.g., \"Esporte Clube\"? \"be\" no. \"Uniao ... GO\"? intended strip. \"Paysandu SC\"? Wait — Paysandu is from Pará (PA)! If data has \"Paysandu SC\"... SC=São Caetano? No, SC=Sergipe? SC = Santa Catarina. Hmm \"Paysandu-PA\" would strip to \"paysandu\" ✓ intended. What real team ends with a bare 2-letter token matching a state? \"Ipatinga\"? one word. I think we're fine.\n\n### Models/MatchRecord.cs\n```csharp\nnamespace BrazilianSoccerMcp.Models;\n\npublic enum VenueFilter { Any, Home, Away }\n\npublic sealed record MatchRecord\n{\n    public required string Competition { get; init; }\n    public required string CompetitionKey { get; init; }\n    public DateTime? Date { get; init; }\n    public required string HomeTeam { get; init; }\n    public required string AwayTeam { get; init; }\n    public required string HomeKey { get; init; }\n    public required string AwayKey { get; init; }\n    public int? HomeGoals { get; init; }\n    public int? AwayGoals { get; init; }\n    public int? Season { get; init; }\n    public int? Round { get; init; }\n    public string? Stage { get; init; }\n    public string? Arena { get; init; }\n    public required string SourceFile { get; init; }\n\n    public bool Played => HomeGoals.HasValue && AwayGoals.HasValue;\n    public int GoalMargin => Played ? Math.Abs(HomeGoals!.Value - AwayGoals!.Value) : 0;\n    public string HomeDisplay => TeamNameNormalizer.DisplayName(HomeTeam);\n    public string AwayDisplay => TeamNameNormalizer.DisplayName(AwayTeam);\n\n    public string FormatLine()\n    {\n        var date = Date?.ToString(\"yyyy-MM-dd\") ?? \"unknown date\";\n        var score = Played ? $\"{HomeGoals}-{AwayGoals}\" : \"vs (no score recorded)\";\n        var round = Stage is { Length: > 0 } ? Stage : Round.HasValue ? $\"Round {Round}\" : null;\n        var suffix = round is null ? $\"({Competition} {Season})\" : $\"({Competition} {Season}, {round})\";\n        return $\"{date}: {HomeDisplay} {score} {AwayDisplay} {suffix}\";\n    }\n}\n```\nHmm \"vs (no score...)\" formatting: better `Flamengo vs Fluminense (no score recorded)`. Let me handle in FormatLine: `Played ? $\"{Home} {hg}-{ag} {Away}\" : $\"{Home} vs {Away} (score unavailable)\"`.\n\n### Models/PlayerRecord.cs\n```csharp\nnamespace BrazilianSoccerMcp.Models;\n\npublic sealed record PlayerRecord\n{\n    public required string Id { get; init; }\n    public required string Name { get; init; }\n    public int? Age { get; init; }\n    public string? Nationality { get; init; }\n    public int? Overall { get; init; }\n    public int? Potential { get; init; }\n    public string? Club { get; init; }\n    public string? ClubKey { get; init; }\n    public string? Position { get; init; }\n    public int? JerseyNumber { get; init; }\n    public string? Height { get; init; }\n    public string? Weight { get; init; }\n    public string? PreferredFoot { get; init; }\n    public Dictionary<string, int> Skills { get; init; } = new();\n    public string NameKey => TextNormalizer.CompactKey(Name);\n}\n```\n\n### Data/CsvParser.cs\nA small record-struct parser:\n```csharp\nnamespace BrazilianSoccerMcp.Data;\n\n/// <summary>Minimal RFC-4180 CSV reader: quoted fields, escaped quotes, embedded newlines.</summary>\npublic static class CsvParser\n{\n    public static IEnumerable<string[]> ReadRows(string path)\n    {\n        using var reader = new StreamReader(path, Encoding.UTF8, detectEncodingFromByteOrderMarks: true);\n        var row = new List<string>();\n        var field = new StringBuilder();\n        var inQuotes = false;\n        var hasContent = false; // field or row started\n        while (true)\n        {\n            var i = reader.Read();\n            if (i < 0) { /* flush */ ... }\n            char c = (char)i;\n            ...\n        }\n    }\n}\n```\nState machine: \n- inQuotes: if c=='\"': peek next; if next=='\"' → append '\"', consume; else inQuotes=false.\n- else: if c=='\"' and field empty-ish (start) → inQuotes=true. Actually quotes can start mid-field in bad data; treat '\"' at field start only (when field.Length==0 && !fieldStarted). Simpler classic: if c=='\"' → toggle? That breaks on `\"\"` handling. Do: if c=='\"': if field.Length==0 && !inQuotes && !started → inQuotes=true, started=true; else append? Hmm. Given clean Kaggle data, standard approach works: quote only at field start.\n\nLet me write it cleanly with a small class:\n\n```csharp\npublic static IEnumerable<string[]> ReadRows(string path)\n{\n    using var reader = new StreamReader(path, Encoding.UTF8, true);\n    var field = new StringBuilder();\n    var row = new List<string>();\n    bool inQuotes = false, fieldStarted = false, rowHasData = false;\n    int ci;\n    while ((ci = reader.Read()) != -1)\n    {\n        char c = (char)ci;\n        if (inQuotes)\n        {\n            if (c == '\"')\n            {\n                if (reader.Peek() == '\"') { reader.Read(); field.Append('\"'); }\n                else inQuotes = false;\n            }\n            else field.Append(c);\n        }\n        else if (c == '\"' && !fieldStarted) { inQuotes = true; fieldStarted = true; rowHasData = true; }\n        else if (c == ',') { row.Add(field.ToString()); field.Clear(); fieldStarted = false; rowHasData = true; }\n        else if (c == '\\r') { /* skip; \\n handles */ }\n        else if (c == '\\n')\n        {\n            row.Add(field.ToString()); field.Clear(); fieldStarted = false;\n            if (rowHasData || row.Count > 1) yield return row.ToArray();\n            row.Clear(); rowHasData = false;\n        }\n        else { field.Append(c); fieldStarted = true; rowHasData = true; }\n    }\n    if (fieldStarted || row.Count > 0) { row.Add(field.ToString()); yield return row.ToArray(); }\n}\n```\n\nWait — a header-only line like \"ID,Data,...\" yields fine. Empty trailing line skipped. CRLF: '\\r' skipped only outside quotes; inside quotes appended (fine).\n\nAlso helper:\n```csharp\npublic sealed class CsvTable\n{\n    public string[] Header; public List<string[]> Rows;\n    public int Column(string name) => index by name (case-insensitive, ordinal)\n    public string? Get(string[] row, string name) => ...\n}\n```\nI'll make CsvTable with a header map and Get(row, col) returning null when missing.\n\n### Data/SoccerDataStore.cs\n\nLoads everything:\n```csharp\npublic sealed class SoccerDataStore\n{\n    public IReadOnlyList<MatchRecord> Matches { get; }\n    public IReadOnlyList<MatchRecord> PrimaryMatches { get; }\n    public IReadOnlyList<PlayerRecord> Players { get; }\n    public IReadOnlyDictionary<string, string> TeamDisplayNames { get; }  // key -> most common display\n    public IReadOnlyDictionary<string, int> CompetitionMatchCounts ...\n    \n    public static SoccerDataStore Load(string dataDirectory) { ... }\n}\n```\n\nLoaders per file with competition key mapping:\n- Brasileirao_Matches.csv → Competition \"Brasileirão Série A\", key \"brasileiraoseriea\" — hmm wait, keys: I decided competition matching = normalized substring. If CompetitionKey is itself a compact key, competition query \"brasileirao\" compacted = \"brasileirao\"; check CompetitionKey.Contains(queryCompact). \"brasileiraoseriea\".Contains(\"brasileirao\") ✓. Historical: key \"brasileiraohistorico\"? Hmm — but then a query \"brasileirao serie a\" compacted \"brasileiraoseriea\" matches only the modern one. And plain \"brasileirao\" matches both modern + historical → good for broad queries; standings pins season anyway and primary view dedups.\n\nWait, subtle: primary view groups by (CompetitionKey, Season). Brasileirao_Matches 2015 and historical 2015 must land in the SAME group to dedup! If keys differ (\"brasileiraoseriea\" vs \"brasileiraohistorico\"), no dedup → double counting in \"all brasileirao\" aggregates? My GetPrimaryMatches groups by (CompetitionKey, Season) — different keys → separate groups → both kept → season appears twice in a full-history aggregate. Is that a problem? For aggregates across all seasons of \"brasileirao\", matches from overlapping sources would double-count.\n\nFix: use a **GroupingKey** distinct from display key. Assign grouping key per logical competition: \"brasileirao-serie-a\" for Brasileirao_Matches + historical + BR-Football \"Serie A\". \"copa-do-brasil\" for cup + BR-Football \"Copa do Brasil\". \"copa-libertadores\". \"brasileirao-serie-b\"/\"-serie-c\". And Competition display per source: \"Brasileirão Série A\", \"Brasileirão (2003–2019)\", \"Brasileirão Série A (BR-Football)\"... hmm display variety complicates \"list_competitions\". \n\nSimpler: unify display too:\n- key \"brasileirao-serie-a\", display \"Brasileirão Série A\" for all three sources (tag source only internally).\n- key \"copa-do-brasil\", display \"Copa do Brasil\".\n- key \"copa-libertadores\", display \"Copa Libertadores\".\n- key \"brasileirao-serie-b\", display \"Brasileirão Série B\".\n- key \"brasileirao-serie-c\", display \"Brasileirão Série C\".\n\nCompetition matching: compact both query and key/display; match if keyCompact.Contains(queryCompact) or displayCompact.Contains(queryCompact). \"brasileirao\" → matches serie a/b/c keys ✓. \"serie a\" → \"seriea\" contained in \"brasileiraoseriea\" ✓. \"libertadores\" ✓. \"copa\" matches all three. Standings tool resolves competition similarly; ambiguity across A/B/C for season 2019: query \"brasileirao\" alone would match 3 competitions — standings should then report per-competition or pick the one with most matches? I'll make standings require reasonably specific competition; if multiple competition keys match, return a message listing them (or compute for each? For B/C it computes fine too). Decide: compute for the best match = the matching competition with most matches that season; if query is exactly \"brasileirao\", prefer Serie A. Implement: order candidates by (query == key ? 0 : 1, matches desc) and if ambiguous (top 2 same matches count and different keys)... overthinking: prefer exact key match; else if \"brasileirao\" → serie-a has most matches typically; pick max matches, note assumption in output. Fine.\n\nSource rank for primary view (per (key, season) group):\n- \"Brasileirao_Matches.csv\" → 0\n- \"Brazilian_Cup_Matches.csv\" → 0\n- \"Libertadores_Matches.csv\" → 0\n- \"novo_campeonato_brasileiro.csv\" → 1\n- \"BR-Football-Dataset.csv\" → 2\n\nOrder by rank ascending, take first group. (Within same rank only one source exists per key anyway.)\n\nTeamDisplayNames: for each key, most frequent DisplayName(raw) across matches (tie → first seen). Used for headers.\n\nLoad fifa: map columns via header names; parse Overall/Potential/Age ints; Skills: pick list [\"Finishing\",\"Dribbling\",\"Crossing\",\"ShortPassing\",\"HeadingAccuracy\",\"BallControl\",\"SprintSpeed\",\"Stamina\",\"Strength\",\"LongShots\",\"GKReflexes\"] if present. Jersey Number int?. Height/Weight raw strings.\n\n### MatchQueryService\n\n```csharp\npublic sealed record MatchQuery\n{\n    public string? Team; public string? Opponent; public string? Competition;\n    public int? Season; public DateTime? From; public DateTime? To;\n    public VenueFilter Venue = VenueFilter.Any;\n    public bool FinalsOnly; public int? Round; public string? Stage;\n    public int Limit = 20;\n}\n```\n\nResolveTeamKeys(query) → (keys, suggestions): exact hit if datastore.TeamDisplayNames contains key. Else substring over display names compact... Implement in datastore: `IReadOnlyList<(string Key, string Display)> FindTeams(string query)`:\n- exact key → [(key, display)]\n- else candidates where CompactKey(display).Contains(q) || q.Contains(CompactKey(display)) → order by display length asc, take 10.\n\nFindMatches: start PrimaryMatches; filter team: keysA = FindTeams(team); if none → return empty + suggestions note. team filter: HomeKey/AwayKey ∈ keysA. opponent similarly on the other side. venue: home → HomeKey ∈ keysA; away → AwayKey ∈ keysA. competition: key match. season equal. From/To on Date (null dates excluded when range given). Round exact. Stage contains (case-insensitive). FinalsOnly: Stage contains \"final\" OR (competition is cup-like and Round == max round for that (key,season)) — compute max round per group on the fly for cup. Simplest: after filtering, if FinalsOnly: group by (CompetitionKey, Season); for groups whose matches have Stage values → keep Stage contains \"final\" (but \"semifinals\" contains \"final\"! use exact \"final\" or endsWith? Libertadores stages: 'final', 'semifinals' — equality check after lowercase trim handles it; also 'quarterfinals' — equality excludes ✓). For numeric-round groups (cup) → keep Round == group max. For Brasileirão league there is no \"final\" — groups with all Round and no Stage: is a league \"final\" meaningful? No — so FinalsOnly applies only to cup/libertadores. How to distinguish? Competition keys \"copa-do-brasil\"/\"copa-libertadores\" are cup-like; serie a/b/c league-like → FinalsOnly yields empty for leagues with a note. Implement CupKeys set {\"copadobrasil\",\"copalibertadores\"} hmm key compact forms: CompactKey(\"copa-do-brasil\") = \"copadobrasil\" ✓.\n\nSort: Date descending (nulls last), then limit.\n\nHeadToHead(teamA, teamB): matches where {HomeKey,AwayKey} pair matches (a,b) either orientation among PrimaryMatches, both resolved. Summary: wins counted from perspective of A; list limited (default 10 most recent + note \"N more\").\n\nTeamRecord: filter team ∈ keys, season?, competition?, venue. Played only for W/D/L. Return also total matches incl. unplayed. GF/GA. WinRate = W/played.\n\nStandings(competition, season): resolve competition → candidates; pick best (prefer exact compact key equality, then most matches that season). Compute rows from played matches. Return rows + chosen competition display + note if ambiguous. Annotate champion/relegation when teams==20 && competition key is serie-a.\n\nHmm — should relegation annotation apply to Série B (promotion instead)? Only annotate Serie A: champion + bottom-4 relegated. Série B: top-4 promoted? skip — keep Serie A only, simple.\n\nGoalStatistics(competition?, season?): over primary played matches filtered; returns MatchesPlayed, TotalGoals, AvgGoals, HomeWins, Draws, AwayWins, HomeWinRate, AvgHomeGoals, AvgAwayGoals.\n\nBiggestWins(competition?, season?, limit): order by margin desc, then total goals desc, then date desc. Include stage/round line.\n\nListCompetitions(): group PrimaryMatches by (CompetitionKey, Competition): matches count, seasons min-max (excluding null), distinct seasons count... return sorted by name.\n\nTeamCompetitions(team): group that team's matches by competition: seasons list + match count.\n\n### PlayerQueryService\n\nPlayerQuery: Name?, Nationality?, Club?, Position?, MinOverall?, Sort (\"overall\"|\"potential\"|\"age\"|\"name\"), Limit.\n- name: normalized contains (both directions? contains is enough: CompactKey(player.Name).Contains(CompactKey(name))).\n- nationality: CompactKey equality first; fallback contains.\n- club: normalized key equality with TeamNameNormalizer.NormalizeKey(player.Club) == NormalizeKey(club); fallback contains on compact display.\n- position: exact code (case-insensitive) or group name mapping.\n- min_overall filter; sort; limit.\nFindByName: exact compact equality → else contains → null + suggestions (contains matches top 5 by overall; if none, first-letter matches?).\n\nPosition groups:\n```csharp\nstatic readonly Dictionary<string, HashSet<string>> PositionGroups = new(StringComparer.OrdinalIgnoreCase)\n{\n    [\"goalkeeper\"] = [\"GK\"],\n    [\"defender\"] = [\"LB\",\"LCB\",\"CB\",\"RCB\",\"RB\",\"LWB\",\"RWB\"],\n    [\"midfielder\"] = [\"LM\",\"LCM\",\"CM\",\"RCM\",\"RM\",\"LDM\",\"CDM\",\"RDM\",\"LAM\",\"CAM\",\"RAM\"],\n    [\"forward\"] = [\"LW\",\"RW\",\"LF\",\"CF\",\"RF\",\"ST\",\"LS\",\"RS\"],\n};\n```\nAlso accept \"attack\"/\"attacker\"→forward, \"mid\"→midfielder? Add aliases \"attacker\"→forward.\n\n### Tools/SoccerTools.cs\n\n[McpServerToolType] public sealed class SoccerTools(MatchQueryService matches, PlayerQueryService players, SoccerDataStore store)\n\n